1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,650
Chapter 6: Interaction: Expanding the Observation and Action Spaces.

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:18,600
Chapter 1 made a claim: when the underlying model is fixed, the most effective system-engineering lever for improving an Agent's task performance is usually to redefine or expand its observation space and action space.

3
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:35,337
Chapters 2 through 5 have explored this idea in practice—context engineering decides what goes into the observation, memory and knowledge bases stretch the observation across sessions, tools define what the Agent can do, and code generation lets it create new actions of its own.

4
00:00:35,337 --> 00:00:42,037
But all of these expansions happened under one shared premise: the Agent and the world take turns speaking.

5
00:00:42,037 --> 00:00:52,025
The user finishes a sentence, the Agent thinks for a while, calls a few tools, and replies; while it is thinking, the world is assumed to stand still.

6
00:00:52,025 --> 00:00:57,012
The premise is so natural that it is rarely written down as an assumption at all.

7
00:00:57,012 --> 00:01:00,400
This chapter removes exactly that premise.

8
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:03,637
Two Axes: Modality and Timing.

9
00:01:03,637 --> 00:01:09,262
Both the observation space and the action space can be expanded along two dimensions.

10
00:01:09,262 --> 00:01:23,737
Modality decides the form of observation and action: does the Agent only read text, or can it also hear sound, see the screen, and sense torque; can it only emit tokens, or also speak, click, and drive joints.

11
00:01:23,737 --> 00:01:39,187
Timing decides the rhythm of observation and action: does the Agent go and fetch an observation, or does the world push it; must an action finish within one turn, or may it span turns, be interrupted midway, and be preempted by something more urgent.

12
00:01:39,187 --> 00:01:46,300
The previous chapters expanded the content of these two spaces; this chapter expands their modality and timing:

13
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:56,250
Expanding the observation space: Context engineering, memory and knowledge bases; Expanding the action space: Tools, code generation.

14
00:01:56,250 --> 00:02:06,212
Expanding the observation space: Voice, screen, physical sensors; Expanding the action space: Speaking, clicking, joint motion.

15
00:02:06,212 --> 00:02:16,562
Expanding the observation space: The world pushes, continuous streams; Expanding the action space: Across turns, interruptible, preemptible.

16
00:02:16,562 --> 00:02:22,862
Turn-taking is an interaction convention of the model and its interface, not a property of the environment.

17
00:02:22,862 --> 00:02:33,475
Early tool-calling interfaces generally organized messages into synchronous rounds: a question was followed by an answer, and tool results had to be supplied before reasoning continued.

18
00:02:33,475 --> 00:02:45,950
The real environment does not wait for the model to react: mail arrives while it is thinking, the user cuts in mid-sentence, a page changes between screenshots, and a cup is knocked over while the arm reaches for it.

19
00:02:45,950 --> 00:02:57,575
This convention is changing too: as of September 2026, GPT-6 Astra offers native asynchronous tool calling and the ability to add user instructions mid-turn.

20
00:02:57,575 --> 00:03:04,150
This chapter therefore covers both native support and compatibility with existing synchronous interfaces.

21
00:03:04,150 --> 00:03:21,037
Scale: Seconds — days; Scenario: Async and event-driven; Change on the observation side: The world wakes the Agent (mail, timers, callbacks); Change on the action side: Actions span turns: start now, finish later on an event.

22
00:03:21,037 --> 00:03:36,837
Scale: 10 ms — 1 s; Scenario: Voice; Change on the observation side: Listen while speaking, without waiting for a full sentence; Change on the action side: Think while speaking, interruptible, revisable midway.

23
00:03:36,837 --> 00:03:51,887
Scale: Sub-second — seconds; Scenario: Computer Use; Change on the observation side: The screen keeps changing between frames; Change on the action side: After acting, reality must be re-confirmed against the plan.

24
00:03:51,887 --> 00:04:05,387
Scale: Milliseconds; Scenario: Robotics; Change on the observation side: Sensors stream back continuously; Change on the action side: Actions are chunked: plan a little at a time, preemptible.

25
00:04:05,387 --> 00:04:09,450
Async and Event-Driven: When the World Comes Looking for You.

26
00:04:09,604 --> 00:04:17,441
The perception, execution, and collaboration tools discussed in Chapter 4 are all invoked proactively by the Agent.

27
00:04:17,391 --> 00:04:22,029
How should an Agent respond to external events that may arrive at any time?

28
00:04:22,029 --> 00:04:25,929
This requires an event-driven asynchronous architecture.

29
00:04:25,929 --> 00:04:35,954
The two remaining tool classes from Chapter 1—event-trigger tools and user-communication tools—depend on this architecture, so they are discussed here as well.

30
00:04:35,954 --> 00:04:42,141
The modality does not change in this section—it is still text; only the timing changes.

31
00:04:42,141 --> 00:04:47,079
This is the first step out of the turn-based world of the previous five chapters.

32
00:04:47,079 --> 00:04:49,616
Why Asynchrony is Needed.

33
00:04:49,616 --> 00:04:54,029
Let's start with an analogy to explain why asynchrony is needed.

34
00:04:54,029 --> 00:05:01,954
Synchronous means "do one thing before you can do the next," while asynchronous means "multiple things can happen concurrently.

35
00:05:01,954 --> 00:05:13,004
A traditional synchronous Agent architecture is like a single checkout counter at a store—it can only handle one customer at a time, and only calls the next number after finishing with the current one.

36
00:05:13,004 --> 00:05:28,729
A truly intelligent assistant is more like a flexible secretary—with multiple pending items on the desk (emails, phone calls, visitors), the secretary decides which to handle first based on urgency, and can pause and switch to a more urgent task mid-way.

37
00:05:28,729 --> 00:05:39,466
In synchronous mode, the Agent either has to wait for a background task to complete before talking to the user, or wait for the conversation to end before processing a newly arrived event.

38
00:05:39,466 --> 00:05:44,579
It cannot deliver the core capabilities a real assistant scenario requires:

39
00:05:44,579 --> 00:05:51,741
Asynchronous execution is the norm—Many tasks require long runtimes and should not block user interaction.

40
00:05:51,741 --> 00:05:56,904
Dynamic judgment of event priority—Not all events are equally important.

41
00:05:56,904 --> 00:06:08,554
The Agent needs to intelligently choose a handling strategy: cancel the current operation (urgent), add it to a queue (routine), or process in parallel (independent lightweight query).

42
00:06:08,554 --> 00:06:15,304
Smooth interruption and resumption—An interrupted conversation or task should be able to resume naturally.

43
00:06:15,304 --> 00:06:22,954
When applying the asynchronous paradigm to an LLM, first check whether the model and API support this message timing.

44
00:06:22,954 --> 00:06:34,041
Some interfaces require all tool results before work can continue; others already allow tools to remain pending while the model keeps working and accepts user updates during generation.

45
00:06:34,041 --> 00:06:42,416
The former need event queues, task handles, and a compatibility layer; the latter can use native asynchronous protocols directly.

46
00:06:42,416 --> 00:06:49,241
Both still require the application to manage event sources, tool lifecycles, and result ownership.

47
00:06:49,241 --> 00:06:55,541
Using asyncio in the code alone does not establish that the model has native asynchronous capabilities.

48
00:06:55,541 --> 00:07:00,316
To solve this, we need an event-driven asynchronous Agent architecture.

49
00:07:00,316 --> 00:07:13,366
Technically, this means the system no longer actively and repeatedly checks for "new messages" (this is polling, which is inefficient), but instead automatically triggers processing logic when a new message arrives.

50
00:07:13,366 --> 00:07:23,841
All inputs, outputs, thought processes, and external interactions are uniformly modeled as an event stream—a sequence of event records arranged on a timeline.

51
00:07:23,841 --> 00:07:35,204
Figure 6-1 shows the overall architecture of an event-driven asynchronous Agent, illustrating the relationship between event sources, the event queue, and the Agent processing flow.

52
00:07:35,204 --> 00:07:41,141
As illustrated in Figure 6-1: Event-Driven Asynchronous Agent Architecture.

53
00:07:41,141 --> 00:07:44,741
Implementing Event-Driven Mechanisms in OpenClaw.

54
00:07:44,741 --> 00:07:52,579
The open-source framework OpenClaw receives multi-channel messages through a Gateway control plane and routes them to the Agent runtime.

55
00:07:52,579 --> 00:07:56,279
It provides three built-in event-driven mechanisms:

56
00:07:56,279 --> 00:08:05,179
Hooks: Respond to events in the Agent's lifecycle, such as session creation and reset, similar to event triggers in GitHub Actions

57
00:08:05,179 --> 00:08:18,566
Cron (scheduled-task scheduler): Execute periodic tasks according to cron expressions (a widely used syntax for scheduled tasks in Unix systems, e.g., 0 9   5 means 9 AM every Friday)

58
00:08:18,566 --> 00:08:25,041
Heartbeat (Heartbeat Daemon): Wakes up the Agent every N minutes to check whether anything requires attention

59
00:08:25,204 --> 00:08:37,479
These three mechanisms give OpenClaw Agents the appearance of autonomy—even with the user offline, the Agent can generate reports on schedule, check system status, and handle routine chores.

60
00:08:37,429 --> 00:08:44,179
The Gateway already handles messages from built-in channels such as IM and the web interface in push fashion.

61
00:08:44,179 --> 00:08:59,891
Of the three mechanisms, only Cron and Heartbeat let the Agent act without a user message, and both are time-driven: Heartbeat checks at fixed intervals, Cron fires at preset times, and Hooks originate inside the OpenClaw framework rather than outside it.

62
00:08:59,891 --> 00:09:09,366
The real gap is third-party event sources beyond the built-in channels: a new email, an external API callback, or an urgent notification.

63
00:09:09,366 --> 00:09:18,179
OpenClaw has no immediate ingress path for them, so the Agent cannot respond immediately and may only notice at the next Cron or Heartbeat tick.

64
00:09:18,179 --> 00:09:21,854
This delay is unacceptable in many scenarios.

65
00:09:21,854 --> 00:09:38,254
Take PineClaw (Pine AI's OpenClaw plugin) as an example: Pine AI is an AI assistant that makes real phone calls on behalf of the user, with typical scenarios including negotiating bills, canceling subscriptions, and handling insurance claims.

66
00:09:38,254 --> 00:09:49,291
When a user initiates a Pine phone task through an OpenClaw Agent, Pine's voice AI will make the call on behalf of the user, but the user may need to intervene at any time during the call:

67
00:09:49,291 --> 00:10:02,254
Real-time Identity Verification: The customer service representative asks to verify the account holder's identity, and Pine needs the user to immediately provide a security code or one-time password (OTP)

68
00:10:02,254 --> 00:10:12,366
Three-Way Call Confirmation: The customer service representative asks to speak directly with the account holder, and Pine needs the user to answer the phone within seconds

69
00:10:12,366 --> 00:10:23,529
Progress Sync and Decision Confirmation: At a critical point in the negotiation (e.g., the other party proposes a price reduction), Pine needs the user to confirm whether to accept

70
00:10:23,529 --> 00:10:34,141
With Heartbeat's periodic polling, the user might not get the notification while the representative is still waiting for the verification code; the representative hangs up and the call fails.

71
00:10:34,141 --> 00:10:42,391
PineClaw's solution is a Channel mechanism that establishes a real-time event path between OpenClaw's Gateway and the Pine API.

72
00:10:42,391 --> 00:10:51,816
When a call connects, needs user input, or ends, the message is pushed immediately to the OpenClaw Agent, which handles it and notifies the user.

73
00:10:51,816 --> 00:11:05,879
This case reveals the core value of an event-driven architecture for Agent frameworks: true "proactive service" requires not only that the Agent can periodically check for events, but also that events can actively notify the Agent.

74
00:11:05,879 --> 00:11:20,891
Unifying all inputs—user messages, tool returns, external callbacks, scheduled triggers—into an event stream, and driving the Agent's thinking and actions through an event loop, is the architectural foundation for achieving this goal.

75
00:11:20,891 --> 00:11:36,979
Under this architecture, we will first introduce the two tool categories directly related to events, as well as the virtual identity and isolated execution environment that support the Agent's independent actions, before discussing the specific design of the event handling mechanism.

76
00:11:36,979 --> 00:11:39,191
Event-Triggered Tools.

77
00:11:39,191 --> 00:11:44,966
Event-triggered tools are the entry points through which external events drive an Agent's actions.

78
00:11:44,966 --> 00:11:55,141
Without them, an Agent can only operate in a continuous loop of thinking, calling tools, and finally outputting a result, then waiting for the user's next input.

79
00:11:55,141 --> 00:12:02,516
To translate changes in the world into events an Agent can process, there are three common types of event-triggered tools.

80
00:12:02,668 --> 00:12:07,830
Timers (set_timer) handle events tied to physical time.

81
00:12:07,780 --> 00:12:19,605
If an email goes unanswered, the Agent should follow up after a while to ask about progress; if a call is placed outside the recipient's business hours, it should retry during the next business window.

82
00:12:19,605 --> 00:12:25,605
Tools like OpenClaw and Claude Code therefore let an Agent wake itself at a specified time.

83
00:12:25,605 --> 00:12:39,868
One-shot timers handle tasks with a specific time: if a user asks on Saturday to “call the bank's mortgage department for a status update,” the Agent sets “call the bank next Monday at 10:00 AM,” and the timer triggers the call.

84
00:12:39,868 --> 00:12:45,518
Recurring timers handle periodic tasks, such as checking server health every hour.

85
00:12:45,518 --> 00:12:52,780
Some external services cannot push progress updates and must be polled; the recurring timer provides that polling.

86
00:12:52,780 --> 00:13:00,255
OpenClaw's Heartbeat is a systematized version of this mechanism and the basis of its “proactive service” capability.

87
00:13:00,255 --> 00:13:08,643
Background Task Monitoring (monitor_shell) handles events from asynchronously executing tools or command-line tasks.

88
00:13:08,643 --> 00:13:15,168
Some command-line tasks run in the background for a long time, and the Agent needs to track their progress.

89
00:13:15,168 --> 00:13:33,605
If the Agent "stares at the command line," repeatedly calling a tool to poll for progress, it burns tokens; if it waits until the task has fully finished before thinking again, it misses critical problems as they unfold—and if the command hangs, it cannot intervene at all, stalling the whole task.

90
00:13:33,605 --> 00:13:43,518
Claude Code solves this by introducing a monitor tool, allowing the Agent to monitor new command-line output, including output that contains specific keywords.

91
00:13:43,518 --> 00:13:53,880
External Event Channels (connect_channel) push external events like new emails, API callbacks, or IM messages to the Agent in real time.

92
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:59,255
The Channel mechanism in PineClaw from the previous section is a typical implementation.

93
00:13:59,255 --> 00:14:10,130
From a design perspective, event-triggered tools should define clear trigger conditions and filtering rules to prevent irrelevant events from waking the Agent and wasting computational resources.

94
00:14:10,130 --> 00:14:18,518
The event payload should contain sufficient context information to minimize the number of additional queries the Agent needs to make after being woken up.

95
00:14:18,518 --> 00:14:21,155
User Communication Tools.

96
00:14:21,155 --> 00:14:27,530
User communication tools arise as communication channels between Agents and users diversify.

97
00:14:27,530 --> 00:14:40,705
Many Agents, such as Claude Code and Manus, use a native ReAct loop: everything the Agent “says” (an assistant message) is sent directly to the user, who must open a specific session in the app to converse with it.

98
00:14:40,705 --> 00:14:44,980
The session often exposes the Agent's tool-call process.

99
00:14:45,148 --> 00:14:47,710
OpenClaw breaks this pattern.

100
00:14:47,660 --> 00:14:59,498
Users do not need to think in terms of individual sessions or follow the details of tool calls; both user and Agent can send messages at any time instead of alternating one request with one response.

101
00:14:59,498 --> 00:15:06,885
This gives OpenClaw what many describe as a “human-like presence”, communicating asynchronously like a secretary.

102
00:15:06,885 --> 00:15:17,885
Rather than sending raw assistant messages, OpenClaw uses dedicated messaging tools whose messages can include images and files and can trigger push notifications based on urgency.

103
00:15:17,885 --> 00:15:28,473
Beyond communicating with users through text, a growing number of Agents have multimodal communication capabilities, such as sending structured card messages or reminder emails.

104
00:15:28,473 --> 00:15:39,398
Some Agents have begun experimenting with Generative UI—using HTML and similar means to generate interactive interfaces that present information to the user in a friendlier way.

105
00:15:39,398 --> 00:15:51,935
At the design level, user communication tools should support asynchronous messaging (the user may not be online), provide read/unread status tracking, and keep messages consistent across channels.

106
00:15:51,935 --> 00:15:55,748
Multi-channel User Communication and Re-engagement.

107
00:15:55,748 --> 00:16:04,123
An Agent's response should not be limited to a single channel; the notification mechanism also serves as a user re-engagement mechanism.

108
00:16:04,123 --> 00:16:12,610
Message sending extends to instant messaging, SMS, email, phone calls, push notifications, and other channels.

109
00:16:12,610 --> 00:16:24,935
The Agent decides on the channel based on a combination of urgency, user status, content nature, and user preferences, ensuring important messages are not missed while avoiding redundant interruptions.

110
00:16:24,935 --> 00:16:32,635
For long-running tasks, the Agent needs to proactively notify the user upon completion to bring the user's attention back.

111
00:16:32,635 --> 00:16:41,098
For periodic tasks (like daily summaries or weekly reports), notifications can help users develop a regular interaction habit.

112
00:16:41,098 --> 00:16:45,923
User communication tools solve the problem of "how to reach the user.

113
00:16:45,923 --> 00:16:58,860
However, the identity the Agent assumes on these channels and the environment in which it performs actions on behalf of the user require a layer of identity and execution-environment infrastructure, which is the topic of the next section.

114
00:16:58,860 --> 00:17:03,060
Virtual Identity and Isolated Execution Environment.

115
00:17:03,060 --> 00:17:11,285
Chapter 4 opens with Samantha in Her as an example, illustrating how an Agent uses tools to interact with the real digital world.

116
00:17:11,285 --> 00:17:21,860
Achieving such a general-purpose assistant forces a key architectural choice: should the Agent manage the user's personal accounts directly, or hold a virtual identity of its own?

117
00:17:21,860 --> 00:17:29,298
Direct management looks convenient, but one Agent error or compromise exposes the user's entire digital identity.

118
00:17:29,298 --> 00:17:46,910
The safer approach is to give the Agent an independent virtual identity—the way a secretary has their own office phone and mailbox—comprising dedicated communication accounts, storage, and computing environments, so the Agent can work on the user's behalf under a transparent, clearly declared identity.

119
00:17:46,910 --> 00:17:52,348
This transparency does not weaken trust; it can make communication more authentic.

120
00:17:52,348 --> 00:17:56,735
Virtual identities need isolated execution environments.

121
00:17:56,735 --> 00:18:08,373
Virtual computers (VMs/containers) and virtual phones (Android emulators) give the Agent operating-system isolation and full desktop or mobile capabilities.

122
00:18:08,373 --> 00:18:17,335
First, a virtual computer can run around the clock regardless of whether the user's device is online and without disrupting the apps the user is operating.

123
00:18:17,335 --> 00:18:23,823
Second, an Agent error can at worst crash the virtual environment rather than the user's real device.

124
00:18:23,823 --> 00:18:29,848
Finally, isolation prevents the Agent from freely accessing the user's local files.

125
00:18:30,004 --> 00:18:34,529
An independent identity also presents two practical challenges.

126
00:18:34,479 --> 00:18:42,966
First, there are anti-bot mechanisms: many websites use CAPTCHAs and IP reputation checks to block automated access.

127
00:18:42,966 --> 00:18:55,079
Virtual environments using data center IPs are easily identified; in practice, normal access often requires configuring a residential proxy network (which uses real household IPs).

128
00:18:55,079 --> 00:19:13,041
Second, access to the user's real accounts: when a task must log in as the user, use Human-in-the-Loop authentication—a VNC/RDP remote desktop where the user logs in personally, sees the full interface the Agent is operating, and understands why authentication is needed.

129
00:19:13,041 --> 00:19:21,454
The session token is then reused within its validity period to avoid interrupting the user repeatedly, balancing autonomy and security.

130
00:19:21,454 --> 00:19:33,316
Data exchange between the Agent and virtual environments uses a shared file system: volume mounts such as /workspace/shared connect the Agent, virtual computer, and virtual phone.

131
00:19:33,316 --> 00:19:38,354
Data is passed by file-path reference rather than copied into context.

132
00:19:38,354 --> 00:19:49,416
For example, a user uploads a CSV to the shared directory; the Agent in the virtual computer analyzes it and saves a chart there; the Agent returns only the chart's path.

133
00:19:49,416 --> 00:19:52,891
Every handoff remains a lightweight path string.

134
00:19:52,891 --> 00:20:06,229
Event-triggered tools allow the world to wake the Agent, user communication tools allow the Agent to reach the user, and virtual identities with isolated execution environments allow the Agent to act independently and auditably.

135
00:20:06,229 --> 00:20:14,041
The remaining question is: when multiple events converge on the same Agent instance simultaneously, how should they be handled?

136
00:20:14,041 --> 00:20:16,466
Event Handling Mechanism.

137
00:20:16,466 --> 00:20:27,579
A single Agent instance may face multiple events concurrently: a new message from the user, a result from a tool, a timer expiring, a collaboration request from another Agent.

138
00:20:27,579 --> 00:20:33,954
How these events are handled efficiently and correctly directly impacts performance and user experience.

139
00:20:33,954 --> 00:20:38,579
The skeleton of this mechanism is the event loop from concurrent programming.

140
00:20:38,579 --> 00:21:01,354
Think of an asynchronous Agent as a long-running loop: each round takes a batch of events off the input queue, appends them to the trajectory, invokes the LLM once, executes the tools it decides to call, then returns to the top of the loop to wait for the next batch of events—the same structure as a Go goroutine reading messages from a channel and processing them round by round inside a for { select { ...

141
00:21:01,354 --> 00:21:07,704
In a traditional synchronous-interface implementation, events are consumed at the boundaries of each round.

142
00:21:07,704 --> 00:21:18,729
While the LLM is reasoning or a tool is executing, new events wait in a queue until the round reaches a safe point—the end of a stretch of reasoning or the return of a tool call.

143
00:21:18,729 --> 00:21:28,729
Native asynchrony allows new requirements to arrive while the model is still thinking or producing output, with the system choosing an appropriate moment to continue processing.

144
00:21:28,729 --> 00:21:32,429
Both have boundaries, managed by different layers.

145
00:21:32,429 --> 00:21:45,379
Cancelling a tool still requires its executor to respond to a cancellation signal, much like checking ctx.Done() in Go; receiving a “stop” message does not itself undo actions that have already occurred.

146
00:21:45,379 --> 00:22:03,466
With this distinction in mind, we first use an event loop compatible with synchronous interfaces to explain three strategies: let events wait for the next natural safe point (queuing), create a safe point early (cancellation), or start another loop without waiting for the main loop's safe point (parallel processing).

147
00:22:03,466 --> 00:22:06,329
We return to native steering later.

148
00:22:06,329 --> 00:22:08,666
Structured Event Modeling.

149
00:22:08,666 --> 00:22:11,416
Handling requires understanding.

150
00:22:11,416 --> 00:22:23,379
A general-purpose Agent's input doesn't come only from the user—a third-party message is not sent by the user to the Agent, yet the Agent must understand it, weigh its importance, and decide whether to step in.

151
00:22:23,379 --> 00:22:28,554
This requires modeling each input as a structured event rich with semantics:

152
00:22:28,554 --> 00:22:34,729
Source (who): The user themselves, a contact, a stranger, a system notification

153
00:22:34,729 --> 00:22:46,379
Channel (how): Phone call, SMS, instant message, email, social media, timer trigger, asynchronous tool call result, command-line monitoring status update

154
00:22:46,540 --> 00:22:53,027
Content (what): Message text, emotional tone, urgency, whether a reply is needed

155
00:22:52,977 --> 00:23:01,065
Context (background): Whether it's a reply to a previous conversation or a new communication, its relevance to the current task

156
00:23:01,065 --> 00:23:06,890
Taking a customer refund request email as an example, the structured event looks like this:

157
00:23:06,890 --> 00:23:11,627
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

158
00:23:11,627 --> 00:23:27,540
Only when these dimensions are clearly modeled as structured events can the Agent maintain a clear understanding in multi-party communication, avoiding mistaking user input for a tool result, or mistaking a tool result containing hidden instructions for a user command (prompt injection).

159
00:23:27,540 --> 00:23:46,240
The complexity of multi-threaded context management also requires the Agent to understand the relationships between multiple conversation threads—how a message from a third party affects the user's mood, the user's role transitions across different conversations, and when to synthesize information from different threads to provide advice.

160
00:23:46,240 --> 00:23:59,902
The trigger ecosystem of workflow platforms such as n8n makes the point: webhooks, timers, email, database changes, file watchers—each trigger is one of the Agent's "senses" for perceiving the world.

161
00:23:59,902 --> 00:24:14,565
Once these heterogeneous events are modeled uniformly in a structured format, the Agent can handle stimuli from different sources in a consistent way; the urgency determination and processing strategies discussed below all rest on this unified modeling.

162
00:24:14,565 --> 00:24:18,302
Dynamic Processing Strategy Based on Urgency.

163
00:24:18,302 --> 00:24:27,690
Humans juggling multiple tasks adapt their strategy to urgency: an emergency makes them drop what they're doing; a routine to-do goes on the list for later.

164
00:24:27,690 --> 00:24:31,727
An Agent's event handling should show the same intelligence.

165
00:24:31,727 --> 00:24:37,965
As illustrated in Figure 6-2: Three Strategies for Asynchronous Event Processing.

166
00:24:37,965 --> 00:24:51,277
Cancellation-Based Processing is used for urgent events; its essence is forcing a safe point early for the urgent event: proactively interrupting the current step to turn this instant into a boundary at which the new event can be consumed.

167
00:24:51,277 --> 00:25:22,665
When an urgent event arrives (e.g., the user clicks "stop" or a supervisory system sends a high-priority instruction): (1) Stop the current operation—if the LLM is reasoning, immediately cancel the streaming response; if a synchronous tool is executing, send a cancel signal; (2) Drain the pending queue by removing all pending events; (3) Append those events together with the urgent event to the end of the trajectory; (4) Immediately re-invoke the LLM with the updated complete trajectory as

168
00:25:22,665 --> 00:25:25,452
input to assess the situation.

169
00:25:25,452 --> 00:25:29,077
For example, if the user inputs "Stop!

170
00:25:29,077 --> 00:25:41,590
I said the wrong thing" while the Agent is about to perform a potentially erroneous operation, the Agent will immediately see this new input, reassess what the user intended, and thus avoid executing the wrong action.

171
00:25:41,590 --> 00:25:45,090
Queued Processing is used for routine events.

172
00:25:45,090 --> 00:26:09,327
When a non-urgent event arrives (e.g., an asynchronous tool returns a result or the user sends supplementary information): (1) Add the event to the end of the queue without interrupting the current operation; (2) Wait for the current operation to complete—let the LLM finish reasoning, let the synchronous tool finish executing; (3) When any tool call completes and returns a tool.result, check the queue.

173
00:26:09,327 --> 00:26:18,390
If the queue is non-empty, append all events to the trajectory at once; (4) The LLM processes the updated trajectory comprehensively.

174
00:26:18,390 --> 00:26:29,590
This enables batch processing, improving efficiency—for example, while the Agent is waiting for a search tool result, the user adds "only show results from the last month.

175
00:26:29,590 --> 00:26:39,502
This supplementary information enters the queue, and when the search results return, both events are presented to the LLM together, avoiding unnecessary round trips.

176
00:26:39,652 --> 00:26:44,202
Parallel Processing is used for independent, lightweight queries.

177
00:26:44,152 --> 00:26:51,739
For example, while the Agent is analyzing a large amount of data, the user suddenly asks, "What's the weather like today?

178
00:26:51,739 --> 00:27:00,877
Such queries have three characteristics: they are unrelated to the main task, require a quick response, and have low execution cost.

179
00:27:00,877 --> 00:27:10,114
Neither cancellation-based (would interrupt the important main task) nor queued processing (would make the user wait too long) is suitable.

180
00:27:10,114 --> 00:27:21,964
The system first assesses the query's independence and complexity, then executes it independently in a parallel reasoning session, calling necessary tools to generate a response and returning it immediately.

181
00:27:21,964 --> 00:27:32,064
The query and response are appended to the main task's trajectory, clearly marked as "executed in parallel with the main task" to avoid confusing the LLM.

182
00:27:32,064 --> 00:27:34,364
Urgency Determination.

183
00:27:34,364 --> 00:27:49,614
Urgent events: User interrupt (user.interrupt), supervisor instruction (supervisor.instruction), inter-Agent interrupt (agent.interrupt), external triggers marked as urgent (e.g., system alerts, payment failures).

184
00:27:49,614 --> 00:28:02,839
Non-urgent events: Regular user input (user.input), Agent input (agent.input), tool results (tool.result), timer triggers (timer.trigger), regular external triggers.

185
00:28:02,839 --> 00:28:18,814
Hardcoded rules have limitations; the semantics of the event dictate the handling method—"Stop immediately!" uses cancellation-based processing, "What's the weather like today?" uses parallel processing, "Send the report in Chinese" uses queued processing.

186
00:28:18,814 --> 00:28:27,652
It is recommended to use a lightweight classification LLM as an event router, quickly determining which strategy to adopt when an event arrives.

187
00:28:27,652 --> 00:28:39,189
A cancellation point must be a position where the tool or the reasoning can wind down safely; an unfinished tool result is represented by an explicit placeholder and must never be faked as a success.

188
00:28:39,189 --> 00:28:48,389
The following experiment, an event-driven email processing Agent, implements the event handling strategies discussed above into a runnable implementation.

189
00:28:48,556 --> 00:28:55,356
Experiment 6-1 advanced difficulty, three stars: : Event-Driven Email Processing Agent

190
00:28:55,306 --> 00:29:01,943
As illustrated in Figure 6-3: Experiment 6-1 Event-Driven Agent Architecture.

191
00:29:01,943 --> 00:29:08,281
This experiment builds the simplest event-driven Agent: an Automated Email Processing Assistant.

192
00:29:08,281 --> 00:29:21,893
The Agent monitors the email inbox, and whenever a new email arrives, it automatically triggers a processing workflow—classification, summarization, draft reply, and notifying the user if necessary.

193
00:29:21,893 --> 00:29:31,731
This is the most intuitive introductory scenario for an event-driven Agent: an external event (new email arrival) triggers a complete Agent thinking cycle.

194
00:29:31,731 --> 00:29:43,118
Experiment Objective: to understand the core idea of event-driven architecture—the Agent no longer waits passively for user input but acts on its own in response to external events.

195
00:29:43,118 --> 00:29:53,481
Through this experiment, readers will master the basic closed loop of event source registration, the event queue, and "event arrives → Agent processes → result delivered".

196
00:29:53,481 --> 00:29:56,218
Event Sources and Event Queue.

197
00:29:56,218 --> 00:30:00,818
The system supports unified access for multiple event sources:

198
00:30:00,818 --> 00:30:10,806
Email Events (on_email_received): Triggered when a new email arrives, either by periodically checking the inbox or receiving push notifications.

199
00:30:10,806 --> 00:30:20,968
IM/SMS Messages (on_im_message, on_sms_message): Triggered by instant messages or SMS messages.

200
00:30:20,968 --> 00:30:31,731
GitHub Events (on_github_pr_update, on_github_issue_update): Triggered by PR review comments or status changes.

201
00:30:31,731 --> 00:30:40,681
Timer Triggers (on_timer_expire): Triggered by scheduled tasks (e.g., daily summaries, weekly report generation).

202
00:30:40,681 --> 00:30:46,743
Webhooks (on_webhook_received): Generic callbacks from external systems.

203
00:30:46,743 --> 00:30:57,356
System Events (on_user_inactive, on_process_timeout, on_resource_alert): Triggered by internal state changes.

204
00:30:57,356 --> 00:31:03,181
All events enter a unified event queue and are processed sequentially in order of arrival.

205
00:31:03,181 --> 00:31:26,806
Each event triggers an independent Agent thinking loop: the Agent reads the event content, calls relevant tools (e.g., querying the knowledge base, reading attachments, searching related email history), generates a processing result (classification labels, summaries, draft replies), and finally either notifies the user via notification tools or directly executes an action.

206
00:31:26,806 --> 00:31:32,143
Validation Scenario: Configure the Agent to monitor a test mailbox.

207
00:31:32,143 --> 00:31:39,518
Simulate receiving three emails—a meeting invitation, a customer complaint, and a marketing advertisement.

208
00:31:39,518 --> 00:31:59,481
The Agent processes them sequentially: for the meeting invitation, it automatically checks for calendar conflicts and drafts an accept/decline reply; for the customer complaint, it extracts key information, marks it as high priority, and notifies the user to handle it; for the marketing advertisement, it automatically archives it.

209
00:31:59,481 --> 00:32:03,393
The entire process requires no user intervention.

210
00:32:03,393 --> 00:32:11,706
Experiment 6-1 demonstrates the simplest event-driven pattern—events enter a queue, and the Agent processes them sequentially.

211
00:32:11,706 --> 00:32:23,043
However, when the Agent needs to respond to interruptions during long-running tool executions, or manage multiple concurrent tasks simultaneously, a simple event queue is insufficient.

212
00:32:23,043 --> 00:32:26,893
Next, we discuss deeper engineering challenges.

213
00:32:26,893 --> 00:32:30,756
Compatibility When Native Asynchrony Is Unavailable.

214
00:32:30,756 --> 00:32:38,718
Experiment 6-1 handles only serial events: events enter the queue, and the Agent processes them one after another.

215
00:32:38,718 --> 00:32:47,943
If the chosen model or interface does not support native asynchrony, a user interruption before a tool returns must be expressed within a synchronous format.

216
00:32:47,943 --> 00:32:55,118
We introduce a compatibility approach here, then discuss GPT-6 Astra's native interface later.

217
00:32:55,118 --> 00:33:01,593
Suppose the Agent is helping a user draft an email and has called a tool to search for contact information.

218
00:33:01,593 --> 00:33:07,768
Before the search returns, the user says, “Wait, first check tomorrow's weather for me.

219
00:33:07,768 --> 00:33:17,643
If the interface requires outstanding tool calls to receive their corresponding results first, the Agent cannot directly process this new message with a call still pending.

220
00:33:17,643 --> 00:33:24,868
This restriction comes from the chosen combination of protocol and model; it is not a rule every LLM must follow.

221
00:33:24,868 --> 00:33:29,281
An Asynchronous Implementation Compatible with a Synchronous Format.

222
00:33:29,452 --> 00:33:41,564
The core idea is: Under normal conditions without interruptions, let the LLM see a standard synchronous trajectory; only when an interruption occurs, insert placeholders to fix the format.

223
00:33:41,514 --> 00:33:44,027
Here are five key rules:

224
00:33:44,027 --> 00:33:50,214
Rule 1: Promptly record completed assistant messages and tool-call items from the API.

225
00:33:50,214 --> 00:33:58,489
Preserve server-managed reasoning state according to the provider's continuation protocol; do not assemble invisible reasoning text yourself.

226
00:33:58,489 --> 00:34:03,502
Rule 2: Record the tool result only when the tool call is complete.

227
00:34:03,502 --> 00:34:08,402
The trajectory is in a "partially completed" state during execution.

228
00:34:08,402 --> 00:34:13,564
Rule 3: Interruptions during tool execution require placeholders.

229
00:34:13,564 --> 00:34:25,202
Generate a placeholder response for the unfinished tool (e.g., "The tool is executing in the background, please prioritize the new event"), append the interruption event, and re-invoke the LLM.

230
00:34:25,202 --> 00:34:30,789
From the LLM's perspective, the assistant message still has a paired tool result.

231
00:34:30,789 --> 00:34:44,314
Rule 4: Without native steering or a supported mid-turn continuation interface, cancel unfinished generation, retain confirmed completed messages and tool state, append the new event, and send a new request.

232
00:34:44,314 --> 00:34:50,514
Do not assume that partial output or hidden reasoning can be freely fed back as a valid prefix.

233
00:34:50,514 --> 00:34:55,539
Rule 5: Non-interrupting events enter the queue for batch processing.

234
00:34:55,539 --> 00:35:00,052
They are appended all at once only after the current cycle is complete.

235
00:35:00,052 --> 00:35:08,627
Using the example of the Agent drafting an email when the user interrupts to ask about the weather, the operation of these five rules is as follows:

236
00:35:08,627 --> 00:35:17,577
The Agent calls search_contacts to search for contact information, and the assistant message is immediately written to the trajectory (Rule 1).

237
00:35:17,577 --> 00:35:23,864
Before the search tool returns results, the user sends "First check tomorrow's weather for me.

238
00:35:23,864 --> 00:35:40,814
Since this is a user interruption, the system generates a placeholder tool result for the unfinished search_contacts ("The tool is executing in the background, please prioritize the new event", Rule 3), then appends the user's weather query to the trajectory and re-invokes the LLM.

239
00:35:40,814 --> 00:35:49,577
At this point, the trajectory format seen by the LLM is completely valid—the assistant message and tool result are perfectly paired.

240
00:35:49,577 --> 00:35:58,764
After the Agent answers the weather query, the original search_contacts result arrives and is appended to the trajectory as a new event (Rule 2).

241
00:35:58,764 --> 00:36:03,589
The Agent reads the contact information and continues drafting the email.

242
00:36:03,589 --> 00:36:09,277
This approach maintains the tool/result pairing required by a synchronous interface.

243
00:36:09,277 --> 00:36:15,277
A placeholder explicitly marked “unfinished” is introduced only when an interruption is needed.

244
00:36:15,277 --> 00:36:21,877
When the real background result arrives, it enters the trajectory as an event with a source and task ID.

245
00:36:21,877 --> 00:36:30,714
For models that already support native asynchrony, the system can retain the task's pending state and pass the real result to the model when it arrives.

246
00:36:30,714 --> 00:36:41,527
Placeholders also carry a semantic risk: the model may confuse “task started” with “task completed” and make a decision that depends on a result before that result arrives.

247
00:36:41,527 --> 00:36:49,927
Clear task state and result validation should prevent this confusion, and evaluation should check for fabricated data that has not yet arrived.

248
00:36:49,927 --> 00:36:55,777
A single failure does not justify attributing the cause to an undisclosed training process.

249
00:36:55,777 --> 00:36:59,714
Expressing Asynchronous Semantics Through Task Handles.

250
00:36:59,714 --> 00:37:07,139
Whether or not a native asynchronous protocol is used, tool-interface design can make asynchronous semantics explicit.

251
00:37:07,139 --> 00:37:14,864
One approach especially useful for synchronous interfaces is to make “start task” a complete call with a real return value.

252
00:37:14,864 --> 00:37:20,177
Traditional tool design implies a "call equals completion" semantics.

253
00:37:20,177 --> 00:37:28,714
For example, the name phone_call suggests "calling will dial the phone and wait for the call to end, returning the call log.

254
00:37:28,714 --> 00:37:34,664
Under the asynchronous paradigm, "initiation" and "completion" should be decoupled:

255
00:37:34,664 --> 00:37:45,502
initiate_phone_call: Initiates a phone call, immediately returning a task identifier and initial status (e.g., "Call initiated, dialing...

256
00:37:45,502 --> 00:37:53,727
Call progress is communicated via event notifications (phone_call_connected, phone_call_ended)

257
00:37:53,884 --> 00:37:59,571
The key is that the tool's name and description themselves should convey asynchronous semantics.

258
00:37:59,521 --> 00:38:09,346
When the model sees initiate_phone_call, its language understanding capabilities will naturally infer this is "initiating" rather than "completing.

259
00:38:09,346 --> 00:38:16,534
The tool description should further reinforce this: "This tool initiates a phone call task handled by a sub-agent.

260
00:38:16,534 --> 00:38:23,571
It returns the task ID immediately upon successful initiation, allowing you to continue with other matters.

261
00:38:23,571 --> 00:38:27,671
A separate notification event will be sent when the call ends.

262
00:38:27,671 --> 00:38:31,409
Overlooking Earlier Events in Queue-Based Processing.

263
00:38:31,409 --> 00:38:38,709
When processing events in a batch, a model may respond only to the last event and overlook earlier requirements.

264
00:38:38,709 --> 00:38:46,909
Native asynchrony addresses whether messages can arrive during execution; we must still check whether the model incorporates all updates.

265
00:38:46,909 --> 00:38:50,046
Intervention can be applied at two levels:

266
00:38:50,046 --> 00:38:58,584
Prompt Level: Inform the model, "When you receive multiple consecutive events, please ensure you comprehensively consider all the information.

267
00:38:58,584 --> 00:39:03,596
Agent Status Bar Markers: Add explicit markers before each event:

268
00:39:03,596 --> 00:39:08,334
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

269
00:39:08,334 --> 00:39:18,096
Add a summary at the end: "There are 4 unprocessed events above, including 1 tool result, 2 user messages, and 1 system reminder.

270
00:39:18,096 --> 00:39:21,721
Please ensure your response covers all the information.

271
00:39:21,721 --> 00:39:30,396
Experiment 6-2 advanced difficulty, three stars: : Asynchronous Agent with Parallel Execution and Interruption Capabilities

272
00:39:30,396 --> 00:39:37,509
As illustrated in Figure 6-4: Experiment 6-2 Asynchronous Agent Interruption and Recovery.

273
00:39:37,509 --> 00:39:50,221
Building on the simple event queue in Experiment 6-1, this experiment uses a runtime compatible with synchronous interfaces to implement parallel tool execution, execution cancellation, and state management.

274
00:39:50,221 --> 00:39:58,346
The Agent must manage several concurrent tasks, handle interruptions and recovery, and make dynamic decisions from current state.

275
00:39:58,346 --> 00:40:02,721
See Experiment 6-3 for the native Astra comparison.

276
00:40:02,721 --> 00:40:14,009
Asynchronous Tool Execution: Supports asynchronous execution of time-consuming tools (at least 3-5 seconds), returning a placeholder immediately upon initiation.

277
00:40:14,009 --> 00:40:19,284
Validation Scenario: The Agent executes a long-running terminal command.

278
00:40:19,284 --> 00:40:23,596
During this time, the user asks, "What time is it now?

279
00:40:23,596 --> 00:40:30,171
The Agent responds immediately, then presents the analysis result when the long-running command completes.

280
00:40:30,171 --> 00:40:37,059
Event Queue and Batch Processing: Accumulates non-urgent events and appends them to the trajectory in a batch.

281
00:40:37,059 --> 00:40:41,859
Validation Scenario: The Agent is executing a long task.

282
00:40:41,859 --> 00:40:49,234
The user sends consecutive messages: "Remember to reply in Japanese" and "Format it as a webpage.

283
00:40:49,234 --> 00:40:56,096
When the task completes, the Agent processes all events at once, generating a Japanese webpage.

284
00:40:56,096 --> 00:41:04,284
Interruption Mechanism: A user's "stop" command immediately terminates the execution flow and cancels the asynchronous tool.

285
00:41:04,284 --> 00:41:09,084
Validation Scenario: The Agent is executing a long task.

286
00:41:09,084 --> 00:41:11,671
The user sends "Cancel.

287
00:41:11,671 --> 00:41:18,396
The Agent stops immediately, and the trajectory records the interruption event and the cancellation operation.

288
00:41:18,396 --> 00:41:28,259
Cancellation and Status Query for Parallel Tools: After an asynchronous tool completes, the real result is injected into the conversation via a new event.

289
00:41:28,259 --> 00:41:32,609
Supports cancellation or progress query via task ID.

290
00:41:32,609 --> 00:41:39,059
Validation Scenario: The user requests, "Run these three scripts simultaneously for me.

291
00:41:39,059 --> 00:41:43,746
Whichever finishes first, check the progress of the remaining scripts.

292
00:41:43,746 --> 00:41:47,534
If any hasn't exceeded 50%, cancel it.

293
00:41:47,534 --> 00:41:58,034
The three scripts simulate analysis processes, outputting progress continuously at speeds of 3%, 2%, and 1% per second, respectively.

294
00:41:58,034 --> 00:42:02,859
The Agent starts three asynchronous terminal commands simultaneously.

295
00:42:02,859 --> 00:42:15,421
When the script at 3% per second finishes in about 33 seconds, the Agent queries the status of the remaining two terminals, finding one at about 66% and the other at about 33%.

296
00:42:15,421 --> 00:42:19,434
It then cancels the one that hasn't exceeded 50%.

297
00:42:19,434 --> 00:42:24,984
After both terminals complete, it integrates the results to generate a complete report.

298
00:42:24,984 --> 00:42:29,321
Model-Native Asynchrony: GPT-6 Astra.

299
00:42:29,476 --> 00:42:38,651
In the compatibility approach above, the runtime orders incoming events so that a model with a synchronous interface can participate in asynchronous tasks.

300
00:42:38,601 --> 00:42:48,751
Another approach lets the model understand this interaction rhythm natively: it can do other work while a tool runs and adjust subsequent work when the user adds requirements midway.

301
00:42:48,751 --> 00:42:57,926
GPT-6 Astra already supports Async tool calling and Mid-turn steering, illustrating this change (Figure 6-5).

302
00:42:57,926 --> 00:43:04,951
As illustrated in Figure 6-5: Synchronous-Interface Compatibility and Model-Native Asynchrony.

303
00:43:04,951 --> 00:43:10,626
Asynchronous tool calling separates “starting an action” from “obtaining its result.

304
00:43:10,626 --> 00:43:19,263
After starting a slow query, an Agent can continue reasoning, call other tools, or handle parts that do not depend on the query result.

305
00:43:19,263 --> 00:43:28,001
While looking up meeting venues, for example, it can prepare the agenda and checklist, then compare options when venue information arrives.

306
00:43:28,001 --> 00:43:36,101
The key is to distinguish dependencies: keep independent work moving, and defer decisions that require the result until it arrives.

307
00:43:36,101 --> 00:43:41,126
Mid-turn steering lets the user correct the direction while a task is underway.

308
00:43:41,126 --> 00:43:50,938
While the Agent is still thinking or composing its answer, the user can add requirements such as “the budget has decreased” or “the number of attendees has changed.

309
00:43:50,938 --> 00:43:59,876
The system retains completed work and brings the new constraints into subsequent processing, allowing the Agent to adjust its plan within the same task.

310
00:43:59,876 --> 00:44:08,926
There may still be a delay between receiving an update and acting on it, but the user need not wait for an entire answer to finish before expressing the change.

311
00:44:08,926 --> 00:44:16,901
These capabilities extend the timing of interaction: tool results and user requirements can both arrive as a task progresses.

312
00:44:16,901 --> 00:44:23,151
The system must still distinguish their sources and remember which work is complete and which remains pending.

313
00:44:23,151 --> 00:44:34,088
Changing a plan does not itself stop running tools or undo actions already taken; actual execution, cancellation, and state management remain the runtime's responsibility.

314
00:44:34,088 --> 00:44:38,063
Not every model has native asynchronous capabilities.

315
00:44:38,063 --> 00:44:50,651
When building an Agent, choose native interaction or a compatibility approach according to the model's support, then check whether the whole system correctly handles delayed results, mid-task changes, and task resumption.

316
00:44:50,651 --> 00:44:58,913
Asynchronous training can improve these capabilities further, but developers can already build this kind of interaction using existing models.

317
00:44:58,913 --> 00:45:04,101
From Receiving Asynchronous Messages to Handling Asynchronous Tasks Reliably.

318
00:45:04,101 --> 00:45:08,913
Native asynchrony addresses whether messages can arrive during execution.

319
00:45:08,913 --> 00:45:14,613
Reliability in complex tasks also depends on how the model uses those messages.

320
00:45:14,613 --> 00:45:17,376
At least three things need checking:

321
00:45:17,524 --> 00:45:26,174
Result ownership and pending state: Can the model associate a delayed result with the correct task and avoid inventing data when the result is absent?

322
00:45:26,124 --> 00:45:35,361
Task resumption and action control: Can it return to the original task after handling new requirements and distinguish changing a plan from stopping execution?

323
00:45:35,361 --> 00:45:43,286
Integrating multiple updates: Can it respect both budget and attendance constraints, rather than remembering only the last message?

324
00:45:43,286 --> 00:45:52,924
These issues can be addressed through model training in asynchronous environments and through clearer task state, event sources, and execution feedback in the system.

325
00:45:52,924 --> 00:46:00,111
Evaluation must cover both layers: whether the model understood the change and whether the system executed accordingly.

326
00:46:00,111 --> 00:46:07,486
Experiment 6-3 advanced difficulty, three stars: : Model-Native Asynchrony and Mid-Turn Steering

327
00:46:07,486 --> 00:46:14,974
Choose a venue for a meeting: after starting a slow query, the Agent completes preparation that does not depend on the result.

328
00:46:14,974 --> 00:46:19,399
Meanwhile, the user adds budget and attendance requirements.

329
00:46:19,399 --> 00:46:24,874
Once the query finishes, the Agent selects a venue according to all constraints.

330
00:46:24,874 --> 00:46:33,474
Call the GPT-6 Astra API to compare synchronous tools, native asynchronous tools, and mid-turn steering.

331
00:46:33,474 --> 00:46:42,424
Observe whether waiting blocks other work, whether new requirements enter subsequent plans, and whether the original task resumes when results arrive.

332
00:46:42,424 --> 00:46:50,261
Then use a model without native support as a control to understand what model capabilities and runtime orchestration each address.

333
00:46:50,261 --> 00:47:00,636
Asynchrony and event-driven execution let the world wake an Agent while a task is underway; native steering also lets the user submit updates before a full answer finishes.

334
00:47:00,636 --> 00:47:12,524
The next three sections compress the timescale further: when the environment changes as fast as or faster than the model generates, receiving updates is not enough—the system must also react in time.

335
00:47:12,524 --> 00:47:16,474
Voice: The Most Natural Human-Machine Interface.

336
00:47:16,474 --> 00:47:19,924
Voice is not merely text turned into sound.

337
00:47:19,924 --> 00:47:31,086
Speaking is roughly four times faster than typing and leaves the hands and eyes free, so it naturally places an Agent in a continuous input-output loop where the user may interrupt at any moment.

338
00:47:31,086 --> 00:47:37,611
Dictation converts speech into text; a voice Agent lets the user collaborate with the Agent directly.

339
00:47:37,611 --> 00:47:41,574
Both support the whisper-coding workflow introduced earlier.

340
00:47:41,574 --> 00:47:49,774
This section covers two directions: the user speaking to an Agent, and an Agent speaking to the outside world on the user's behalf.

341
00:47:49,774 --> 00:48:02,361
The voice model determines what the Agent can answer; the interaction architecture determines whether it can hear clearly, respond in time, hand over naturally, and complete confirmations and tool calls during a call.

342
00:48:02,361 --> 00:48:08,049
We first examine interaction timing, then cognitive timing and expressive quality.

343
00:48:08,049 --> 00:48:12,149
Interaction timing: from cascaded to full-duplex.

344
00:48:12,149 --> 00:48:20,961
OpenAI's GPT-Live introduction describes three voice-interaction paradigms—cascaded, turn-based, and full-duplex.

345
00:48:20,961 --> 00:48:28,261
They are not a simple old-to-new replacement; they trade latency, cost, and observability in different ways:

346
00:48:28,261 --> 00:48:44,449
Paradigm: Cascaded; Core structure: VAD → ASR → LLM → TTS; Main advantage: Clear modules that are easy to replace and debug; Main limitation: Latency accumulates and paralinguistic information is lost at interfaces.

347
00:48:44,449 --> 00:49:02,349
Paradigm: End-to-end Omni; Core structure: Native audio input and output with turn-based interaction; Main advantage: Lower latency and better preservation of tone, emotion, and ambient sound; Main limitation: Still turn-based; training and debugging cost more.

348
00:49:02,349 --> 00:49:21,424
Paradigm: Full-duplex; Core structure: Native audio input and output with continuous listening, speaking, and decision-making; Main advantage: Overlapping speech, natural interruption, and continuous streams; Main limitation: Training, control, and evaluation are more complex.

349
00:49:21,424 --> 00:49:29,261
The common thread is escaping the assumption that people must speak one at a time, and escaping VAD's guess about who has the floor.

350
00:49:29,261 --> 00:49:37,511
Cascaded and Omni systems still divide interaction into turns; full-duplex makes turn ownership a continuous model decision.

351
00:49:37,511 --> 00:49:40,649
Paradigm 1 · Cascaded pipeline.

352
00:49:40,804 --> 00:49:55,654
Most commercial voice assistants still use a serial pipeline (Figure 6-6): VAD decides when the user has finished, ASR converts audio to text, the LLM understands and generates a reply, and TTS speaks it.

353
00:49:55,604 --> 00:50:02,204
Modularity lets each component be optimized independently, but every boundary can add waiting time.

354
00:50:02,204 --> 00:50:07,579
As illustrated in Figure 6-6: Serial voice Agent pipeline.

355
00:50:07,579 --> 00:50:17,279
Module: VAD; Role: Decide whether speech has ended; Typical bottleneck: Silence thresholds add waiting and split turns incorrectly.

356
00:50:17,279 --> 00:50:26,341
Module: ASR; Role: Convert audio to text; Typical bottleneck: Recognition latency and loss of context.

357
00:50:26,341 --> 00:50:35,904
Module: LLM; Role: Understand, reason, and generate; Typical bottleneck: Time to first token; reasoning adds more waiting.

358
00:50:35,904 --> 00:50:44,704
Module: TTS; Role: Convert text to speech; Typical bottleneck: First-packet synthesis and playback buffering.

359
00:50:44,704 --> 00:50:54,716
For a short reply without reasoning, VAD, ASR, LLM, and TTS waiting time accumulates serially (Figure 6-7).

360
00:50:54,716 --> 00:51:00,879
The real value depends on input length, model, hardware, network, and load.

361
00:51:00,879 --> 00:51:06,929
As illustrated in Figure 6-7: Latency waterfall for a serial response.

362
00:51:06,929 --> 00:51:15,229
Production queueing amplifies idle latency further (Figure 6-8), but capacity planning is outside this chapter's scope.

363
00:51:15,229 --> 00:51:20,116
As illustrated in Figure 6-8: Queueing latency curve.

364
00:51:20,116 --> 00:51:26,691
Experiment 6-4 introductory difficulty, one star: : Build a traditional voice Agent

365
00:51:26,691 --> 00:51:37,366
Connect a microphone, Silero VAD, local Whisper, a streaming LLM, and Fish S1 TTS over WebSocket to establish the cascaded baseline.

366
00:51:37,366 --> 00:51:40,229
From serial to streaming perception.

367
00:51:40,229 --> 00:51:49,341
Figure 6-7 describes the fully serial case: VAD, ASR, LLM, and TTS run one after another.

368
00:51:49,341 --> 00:51:53,054
This serial perception approach has three problems:

369
00:51:53,054 --> 00:51:58,416
Accumulated latency: it must wait through silence before confirming the end.

370
00:51:58,416 --> 00:52:07,854
Lost information: a binary speech/no-speech signal cannot express hesitation, emotion, backchannels, or ambient sound.

371
00:52:07,854 --> 00:52:15,304
Broken context: email addresses, names, and proper nouns may be split across chunks and misrecognized.

372
00:52:15,304 --> 00:52:25,116
To address this while keeping the modular split, one optimization is streaming perception, which lets each stage produce incremental results as early as possible:

373
00:52:25,116 --> 00:52:40,379
Streaming ASR: once VAD detects that the user has started speaking, the ASR model is called at fixed intervals to produce a provisional transcript in a streaming fashion; once VAD detects that the user has finished, the final text is confirmed.

374
00:52:40,379 --> 00:52:47,204
LLM speculative execution: the provisional transcript is sent to the LLM as soon as it exists.

375
00:52:47,204 --> 00:52:57,454
If the final text matches the provisional transcript, the LLM is not called again; otherwise the earlier speculative thinking is cancelled and the LLM is called again.

376
00:52:57,454 --> 00:53:04,504
Segmented LLM output: the first speakable sentence goes to TTS without waiting for the full reply.

377
00:53:04,504 --> 00:53:12,929
Incremental TTS: audio chunks are returned continuously so later generation, synthesis, and playback overlap.

378
00:53:12,929 --> 00:53:16,941
A truly streaming ASR needs model-level support.

379
00:53:16,941 --> 00:53:25,604
Whisper's decoder is autoregressive, but its encoder expects a complete audio segment, so it cannot simply be equated with a streaming model.

380
00:53:25,604 --> 00:53:35,191
An LLM-based streaming-audio model can emit text and semantic events from continuous audio, placing recognition and part of understanding in one model.

381
00:53:35,191 --> 00:53:43,641
It keeps the conversation context from the beginning up to the present moment and can use world knowledge for brands, names, and proper nouns.

382
00:53:43,641 --> 00:53:50,441
If the only goal is deciding whether the user has finished, endpointing can be built into the streaming recognizer.

383
00:53:50,441 --> 00:53:55,641
The model combines semantics and silence to judge whether an utterance is complete.

384
00:53:55,641 --> 00:54:04,166
Training labels must contain only information visible at decision time, or hindsight will produce a judgment that cannot be reproduced online.

385
00:54:04,166 --> 00:54:08,316
The model can emit acoustic-event markers as well as words:

386
00:54:08,316 --> 00:54:14,466
speak_start/end, interrupt: speech boundaries and interruption intent;

387
00:54:14,466 --> 00:54:17,829
emotion: emotion and hesitation;

388
00:54:17,829 --> 00:54:23,091
laugh, sigh, noise: paralinguistic and environmental sound.

389
00:54:23,091 --> 00:54:27,754
Together with text tokens, these markers form one event stream.

390
00:54:27,754 --> 00:54:35,516
The Agent can detect hesitation, interruption, and environmental changes without compressing every sound into plain text.

391
00:54:35,668 --> 00:54:43,955
Experiment 6-5 introductory difficulty, one star: : Simulate streaming voice perception with Qwen2-Audio

392
00:54:43,905 --> 00:54:47,855
Qwen2-Audio is not itself a streaming model.

393
00:54:47,855 --> 00:54:56,555
This experiment simulates continuous perception with increasing audio prefixes and compares it with 600 ms VAD + Whisper.

394
00:54:56,555 --> 00:55:00,780
Paradigm 2 · End-to-end omnimodal models (Omni).

395
00:55:00,780 --> 00:55:13,330
Even with streaming perception, a cascade passes listening, thinking, and speaking through discrete interfaces; emotion, intonation, and ambient sound may be lost when audio becomes plain text.

396
00:55:13,330 --> 00:55:24,843
The Omni approach uses one model to listen to audio, generate a reply, and speak it, which can preserve those signals, though at a higher training cost (Figure 6-9).

397
00:55:24,843 --> 00:55:34,230
Compared with the cascaded pipeline of Paradigm 1, Omni's advantage shows up mainly in latency and in understanding and generating non-text information.

398
00:55:34,230 --> 00:55:39,368
On the understanding side, Omni models can pick up on pauses in the voice.

399
00:55:39,368 --> 00:55:48,280
On the generation side, Omni models can convey richer paralinguistic information—singing, or delivering a line in a distinctive tone.

400
00:55:48,280 --> 00:55:54,093
Omni models still assume turn-taking and generally use VAD to assign the floor.

401
00:55:54,093 --> 00:56:00,843
A mid-utterance pause while the user reads out a string of digits can therefore still be mistaken for the end of the turn.

402
00:56:00,843 --> 00:56:07,218
As illustrated in Figure 6-9: End-to-end omnimodal speech-model comparison.

403
00:56:07,218 --> 00:56:17,205
Experiment 6-6 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Run MiniCPM-o 4.5 locally—end-to-end versus self-cascade

404
00:56:17,205 --> 00:56:28,518
Run MiniCPM-o 4.5 locally with thinking mode disabled, comparing direct answers from audio against a self-cascade that first transcribes and then answers with the same model.

405
00:56:28,518 --> 00:56:35,080
This measures whether audio information is preserved, not the “thinking while speaking” discussed later.

406
00:56:35,080 --> 00:56:38,943
Paradigm 3 · Full-duplex interactive models.

407
00:56:38,943 --> 00:56:48,780
Omni still divides conversation into “the user speaks” and “the model speaks,” but simultaneous interpreting and similar tasks require overlap.

408
00:56:48,780 --> 00:56:59,818
A full-duplex model therefore does not presuppose turns: it listens and speaks continuously and repeatedly decides whether to continue, pause, interrupt, or call a tool.

409
00:56:59,818 --> 00:57:04,930
Kyutai's Moshi (2024) was an early research example.

410
00:57:04,930 --> 00:57:12,905
It models the user's and the model's audio streams in parallel, so overlapping speech and interruption can be natural behaviors.

411
00:57:12,905 --> 00:57:16,730
Thinking Machines Lab calls this an Interaction Model

412
00:57:16,730 --> 00:57:29,955
OpenAI's GPT-Live brings the full-duplex path to production scale: it continuously processes input and generates output, can wait, backchannel, be interrupted, and handle realtime translation.

413
00:57:29,955 --> 00:57:37,505
Like the Interaction Model, it delegates complex work to a background model while the foreground model maintains the conversation.

414
00:57:37,505 --> 00:57:41,805
Cognitive timing: realtime interaction and deep thinking.

415
00:57:41,805 --> 00:57:46,293
Interaction quality and intelligence ceiling are different dimensions.

416
00:57:46,293 --> 00:57:53,143
The foreground model must respond while the user is still engaged; the background model can spend longer thinking.

417
00:57:53,143 --> 00:57:57,793
The following three designs are trade-offs, not a linear progression.

418
00:57:57,793 --> 00:58:06,230
The first two can wrap a cascade or Omni model; the third instead unifies deep reasoning and realtime expression within the same model.

419
00:58:06,230 --> 00:58:11,393
Solution 1: Fast thinking for fillers, slow thinking for answers.

420
00:58:11,393 --> 00:58:19,068
Fast thinking can give a holding response within a few hundred milliseconds while slow thinking performs a deeper derivation in the background.

421
00:58:19,068 --> 00:58:30,243
Simple questions may be processed twice, while hard questions can produce contradictions: the fast model recommends a purchase, then the slow model discovers that a key feature is missing.

422
00:58:30,243 --> 00:58:34,555
The root cause is two independent instances thinking separately.

423
00:58:34,555 --> 00:58:41,618
As illustrated in Figure 6-10: Fast/slow thinking architecture and design alternatives.

424
00:58:41,618 --> 00:58:47,155
Solution 2: Fast thinking for interaction, slow thinking for advice.

425
00:58:47,308 --> 00:58:56,345
The background model can send advice through a status bar or dedicated interface while the foreground model keeps the conversation alive and decides how to phrase it.

426
00:58:56,295 --> 00:59:06,245
This is more stable than Solution 1, but communication is still indirect: the foreground can misunderstand the advice and cannot see the background's intermediate reasoning.

427
00:59:06,245 --> 00:59:11,833
Before the background finishes, follow-up questions still rely on the foreground model.

428
00:59:11,833 --> 00:59:16,858
It can naturally wait for a result, but it cannot truly think while speaking.

429
00:59:16,858 --> 00:59:21,670
Solution 3: End-to-end unification of thinking and expression.

430
00:59:21,670 --> 00:59:26,795
This design internalizes reasoning directly in an end-to-end audio model.

431
00:59:26,795 --> 00:59:41,145
Step-Audio R1 uses two complementary mechanisms: Modality-Grounded Reasoning Distillation (MGRD) grounds thinking in acoustic features, while the MPS dual-brain architecture lets planning and expression proceed in parallel.

432
00:59:41,145 --> 00:59:46,045
The first helps the model think correctly; the second helps it speak in time.

433
00:59:46,045 --> 00:59:53,045
Ideally, the model infers emotion from pitch, rhythm, and intonation rather than only from the transcript.

434
00:59:53,045 --> 01:00:02,533
MGRD selects reasoning traces that actually cite acoustic features, trains on them, and uses reinforcement learning to prevent guessing without thinking.

435
01:00:02,533 --> 01:00:12,420
MPS lets the planning brain continuously emit thought segments; the expression brain combines each segment with the partial reply and immediately generates speech.

436
01:00:12,420 --> 01:00:19,845
The pipeline runs in parallel, so the listener need not wait for the entire chain of reasoning before hearing the first sentence.

437
01:00:19,845 --> 01:00:25,295
The trade-off between separated fast/slow thinking and end-to-end reasoning.

438
01:00:25,295 --> 01:00:33,558
A unified model implements “thinking while speaking” most directly, but thinking and realtime expression must be retrained together.

439
01:00:33,558 --> 01:00:37,933
A decoupled design makes it easier to swap the background brain.

440
01:00:37,933 --> 01:00:41,608
These are trade-offs, not simple substitutes.

441
01:00:41,608 --> 01:00:53,583
As frontier reasoning models advance rapidly, separating fast and slow thinking offers an important engineering advantage: it captures the gains from each new generation of slow models directly.

442
01:00:53,583 --> 01:01:04,970
The fast foreground model only needs to listen, respond, and sustain the conversation with low latency, while the slow background model handles reasoning, planning, and tool use.

443
01:01:04,970 --> 01:01:14,083
When a stronger reasoning model arrives, only the background model needs to be replaced; the entire realtime voice system need not be retrained.

444
01:01:14,083 --> 01:01:24,695
A unified design binds reasoning and interaction to the same training cycle, so every upgrade must rebalance intelligence, response latency, and natural expression.

445
01:01:24,695 --> 01:01:35,420
Fast/slow separation is therefore not merely a compromise on latency, but a modular choice that lets interaction capability and the intelligence ceiling evolve independently.

446
01:01:35,420 --> 01:01:40,158
This separation does not necessarily sacrifice task performance.

447
01:01:40,158 --> 01:01:56,233
As of August 2026, Pine AI's voice Agent, which uses a separated fast/slow architecture, ranked first on the τ³-Voice Leaderboard, ahead of realtime voice systems including Grok Voice and GPT-Realtime-2.

448
01:01:56,233 --> 01:02:06,933
At minimum, this result shows that a decoupled architecture is not inherently inferior to end-to-end models on tasks that jointly test deep reasoning and realtime conversation.

449
01:02:07,084 --> 01:02:13,771
The term “end-to-end model” needs one further clarification because it is commonly used in two senses.

450
01:02:13,721 --> 01:02:24,784
The first is an end-to-end speech path, discussed in the preceding section: the model receives audio and produces audio directly instead of connecting multiple models through discrete text.

451
01:02:24,784 --> 01:02:37,384
Both Omni and Interaction Models are end-to-end in this sense, but Omni models usually remain turn-based, whereas Interaction Models can listen and speak at the same time; their architectures differ substantially.

452
01:02:37,384 --> 01:02:51,459
The second is an end-to-end cognitive architecture, discussed in this section: realtime interaction and deep reasoning either share state and are trained together within one model, or are split between a fast foreground model and a slow background model.

453
01:02:51,459 --> 01:02:54,109
The two axes are independent.

454
01:02:54,109 --> 01:03:07,146
A system can have an end-to-end speech path while retaining fast/slow separation in its cognitive architecture; Thinking Machines Lab's delegation of complex tasks to a background reasoner is one such combination.

455
01:03:07,146 --> 01:03:09,996
More human-like speech synthesis.

456
01:03:09,996 --> 01:03:15,509
Traditional TTS can sound artificial when it is too smooth and pauses too little.

457
01:03:15,509 --> 01:03:22,196
Pauses, filler words, and occasional repetition signal uncertainty and thought in human speech.

458
01:03:22,196 --> 01:03:38,084
The main LLM can emit control markers in addition to text, such as THINKING, EMO:happy, and SPEED:0.8x; TTS maps them to pauses, prosody, speaking rate, laughter, sighs, and other nonverbal audio.

459
01:03:38,084 --> 01:03:46,784
The implementation can be a TTS trained to understand control markers, or voice cloning with reference clips for different emotions and styles.

460
01:03:46,784 --> 01:03:54,559
Experiment 6-7 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Control token-driven TTS with Fish Audio

461
01:03:54,559 --> 01:04:05,659
Use Fish Audio S1 to build a multi-reference voice library and compare three configurations: no control markers, one reference clip, and multiple reference clips.

462
01:04:05,659 --> 01:04:11,821
The execution layer selects matching emotion, speaking rate, and style from the markers.

463
01:04:11,821 --> 01:04:15,709
Computer Use: GUI Automation Agents.

464
01:04:15,709 --> 01:04:22,834
Voice pushed the timing axis down to the millisecond, but its observation is still a one-dimensional stream of sound.

465
01:04:22,834 --> 01:04:33,421
Computer Use moves the same problem onto a two-dimensional screen: observation becomes continuously changing pixels, and action becomes clicks and keystrokes at coordinates.

466
01:04:33,421 --> 01:04:46,671
Voice scenarios emphasize when to speak; Computer Use emphasizes where to click next—plus a question that does not exist in voice interaction at all: after an action executes, is reality still consistent with the plan?

467
01:04:46,671 --> 01:05:04,971
Computer Use, also known as GUI automation, allows AI to use software like a human by observing the screen and operating the mouse and keyboard—for example, opening a browser to search for information, filling in data in a spreadsheet application, or adjusting configurations in system settings.

468
01:05:04,971 --> 01:05:09,434
Its core is a Perceive-Think-Act loop (Figure 6-11

469
01:05:09,434 --> 01:05:12,884
The Agent takes a screenshot of the current screen.

470
01:05:12,884 --> 01:05:19,834
A multimodal model receives the screenshot and task instruction, and outputs a thought and a specific action.

471
01:05:19,834 --> 01:05:27,809
The execution layer performs the action in the real environment (moving the mouse, clicking, typing text, etc.).

472
01:05:27,809 --> 01:05:34,096
It waits for the interface to respond, takes another screenshot, and enters the next loop iteration.

473
01:05:34,096 --> 01:05:39,009
It is important to distinguish understanding the interface from completing the task.

474
01:05:39,009 --> 01:05:45,696
The former is closer to multimodal understanding and can be measured with one-shot screenshot question answering.

475
01:05:45,696 --> 01:05:56,559
The latter requires the model to put understanding and action generation into a closed loop that handles page loading, state changes, mistakes, and irreversible consequences.

476
01:05:56,559 --> 01:06:05,934
The challenge of Computer Use is therefore not merely answering correctly about a screenshot, but reconfirming after every step that reality still matches the plan.

477
01:06:05,934 --> 01:06:11,884
As illustrated in Figure 6-11: Computer Use Agent's Perceive-Think-Act Loop.

478
01:06:11,884 --> 01:06:26,871
There are three key design dimensions in this loop: Action Space (what operations the Agent can perform), Visual Grounding (how to find the target element in the screenshot), and Model Architecture (how to generate the correct action from the screenshot).

479
01:06:26,871 --> 01:06:29,196
Action Space Design.

480
01:06:29,356 --> 01:06:37,656
Anthropic's reference implementation divides a complete interaction capability into three types of tools (Figure 6-12).

481
01:06:37,606 --> 01:06:59,043
This is a clear action-space design, but not a private protocol that model providers must follow: as long as the Harness can translate the same screenshots, action constraints, and execution results into messages and structured outputs supported by the target model, Claude, open-weight vision models, and self-hosted endpoints can all drive the same Perceive-Think-Act loop.

482
01:06:59,043 --> 01:07:04,518
As illustrated in Figure 6-12: Computer Use Action Space.

483
01:07:04,518 --> 01:07:24,806
GUI Operation Tool (computer tool): Mouse operations include moving (mouse_move), left/right/middle clicks, double-clicking or triple-clicking, dragging (left_click_drag), and more precise press/release actions (left_mouse_down and left_mouse_up).

484
01:07:24,806 --> 01:07:31,031
Scrolling (scroll) supports four directions and can be combined with modifier keys.

485
01:07:31,031 --> 01:07:45,981
Keyboard operations include typing character by character (type, with a 12ms interval between characters to simulate real typing), key combinations (key, e.g., Ctrl+C), and holding a key (hold_key).

486
01:07:45,981 --> 01:07:54,406
Perception actions include taking a screenshot, retrieving the cursor position (cursor_position), and waiting (wait).

487
01:07:54,406 --> 01:08:01,993
Command Execution Tool (bash tool): Provides a persistent bash terminal session with a 120-second timeout.

488
01:08:01,993 --> 01:08:13,243
It uses a sentinel string to detect command completion and maintains environment state across multiple calls (e.g., after cd to a directory, the next call remains in that directory).

489
01:08:13,243 --> 01:08:24,943
File Editing Tool (str_replace_editor): Enables safe editing through string matching and supports view, create, replace, insert, and undo operations.

490
01:08:24,943 --> 01:08:31,893
It is more precise than overwriting an entire file and less likely to modify unrelated content accidentally.

491
01:08:31,893 --> 01:08:40,906
Experiment 6-8 introductory difficulty, one star: : Running Computer Use (Anthropic Reference Path or Open-Model Path)

492
01:08:40,906 --> 01:08:44,493
Path A uses the Anthropic Computer Use Demo.

493
01:08:44,493 --> 01:08:52,481
Its container packages a complete Ubuntu desktop environment, including a browser, terminal, and other common tools.

494
01:08:52,481 --> 01:09:03,543
The frontend receives a task, while the backend sends the instructions and screenshots to Claude and then executes the mouse, keyboard, terminal, or editing actions returned by the model.

495
01:09:03,543 --> 01:09:08,856
Path B uses the example code in chapter6/computer-use-open-model.

496
01:09:08,856 --> 01:09:21,893
By default, it drives browser-use with the open-weight Qwen3-VL 32B Instruct model through the hosted OpenRouter API, or through self-hosted vLLM/SGLang and similar systems.

497
01:09:21,893 --> 01:09:23,893
Visual Grounding.

498
01:09:23,893 --> 01:09:31,581
In each iteration of the loop, the model needs to accurately locate the target element in the screenshot—"Where is the search box?

499
01:09:31,581 --> 01:09:34,418
What are the coordinates of the submit button?

500
01:09:34,418 --> 01:09:37,256
This is the visual grounding problem.

501
01:09:37,256 --> 01:09:56,043
Currently, there are two main approaches: one is to turn localization into a multiple-choice problem—first annotate the interface elements with numbers, and the model only needs to select one; the other is pure coordinate prediction—letting the model "look" at the screenshot and report coordinates directly, just like a human.

502
01:09:56,043 --> 01:10:13,593
The multiple-choice approach has two implementation methods: pure visual annotation (the original Set-of-Mark, using a segmentation model to segment candidate regions in the image) and structured element indexing (DOM/Accessibility Tree, directly reading the interface's inherent structure).

503
01:10:13,593 --> 01:10:26,331
The common advantage of the multiple-choice approach is that it transforms the open-ended problem of "find the button in the screenshot and predict its coordinates" into a closed-ended one of "choose one from the already annotated elements.

504
01:10:26,331 --> 01:10:42,043
Just as multiple-choice questions are easier to answer correctly than fill-in-the-blank questions in an exam, the model only needs to say "click [123]" instead of "click the button at screen coordinates (350, 464).

505
01:10:42,043 --> 01:10:51,631
Predicting coordinates directly is especially hard for a model—it takes extensive training to get right, and it is prone to error across different screen resolutions.

506
01:10:51,631 --> 01:10:55,143
Set-of-Mark: Visual Annotation Method.

507
01:10:55,300 --> 01:11:06,462
The original Set-of-Mark (SoM) was proposed by Microsoft Research in 2023, initially to unlock the visual grounding capabilities of GPT-4V.

508
01:11:06,412 --> 01:11:21,150
It is a purely visual method: it uses image segmentation models (SAM, SEEM, etc.) to automatically segment candidate regions in the screenshot, overlays a numbered marker on each region, and the model sees an image with numbers.

509
01:11:21,150 --> 01:11:28,237
The model only needs to report the number, and the system converts it into the center coordinates of the corresponding region.

510
01:11:28,237 --> 01:11:42,025
The entire process does not require a DOM or any internal interface structure, so it is equally applicable to native desktop software and game interfaces—as long as the segmentation model can identify the candidate regions.

511
01:11:42,025 --> 01:11:47,637
Structured Element Indexing: A Structured Implementation of the SoM Idea on the Web.

512
01:11:47,637 --> 01:11:53,325
When the interface itself provides structured information, annotation can be more precise.

513
01:11:53,325 --> 01:12:04,362
Before rendering, modern web pages define a complete element structure (the DOM tree) and semantic roles that identify buttons, input fields, and other controls.

514
01:12:04,362 --> 01:12:09,962
Accessibility trees provide similar information for many desktop applications.

515
01:12:09,962 --> 01:12:17,650
Web Agent systems such as browser-use take exactly this route: they enumerate and number interactive elements from the DOM.

516
01:12:17,650 --> 01:12:23,737
This is a structured implementation of the SoM idea for the web (Figure 6-13).

517
01:12:23,737 --> 01:12:26,412
The process has four steps:

518
01:12:26,412 --> 01:12:37,062
Obtain the structured representation (DOM tree) and accessibility information for the page through the browser's debugging interface (CDP, Chrome DevTools Protocol)

519
01:12:37,062 --> 01:12:44,075
Automatically detect which elements are interactive (buttons, input boxes, links, etc.)

520
01:12:44,075 --> 01:12:50,175
Annotate each interactive element with a unique ID and draw bounding boxes on the screenshot

521
01:12:50,175 --> 01:12:55,862
Simultaneously generate a text list describing the element corresponding to each ID

522
01:12:55,862 --> 01:13:00,600
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

523
01:13:00,600 --> 01:13:07,575
The model only needs to output an ID, and the system automatically clicks the center of the corresponding element.

524
01:13:07,575 --> 01:13:20,925
This approach does not save tokens because all annotation data must still be sent to the model, but it provides accurate, stable localization while avoiding the missed detections and false positives that segmentation models can introduce.

525
01:13:20,925 --> 01:13:28,975
As illustrated in Figure 6-13: Set-of-Mark vs. Structured Element Indexing (browser-use implementation).

526
01:13:28,975 --> 01:13:31,400
Pure Coordinate Prediction.

527
01:13:31,400 --> 01:13:36,900
The third route skips annotation and asks the model to output coordinates directly.

528
01:13:36,900 --> 01:13:46,312
Systems such as SeeClick and Claude's computer use rely on vision models trained on massive datasets of GUI screenshots paired with element positions.

529
01:13:46,312 --> 01:13:57,775
These models learn to map natural-language descriptions (e.g., "click the submit button") directly to precise screenshot coordinates, relying on visual perception much like a human user.

530
01:13:57,940 --> 01:14:07,427
In coordinate prediction schemes, the model's understanding of coordinates is highly dependent on the resolution used during training (Figure 6-14).

531
01:14:07,377 --> 01:14:19,877
Claude was trained using XGA (1024×768), WXGA (1280×800), and FWXGA (1366×768).

532
01:14:19,877 --> 01:14:31,452
If the input screenshot resolution does not match, the model's predicted coordinates will systematically shift—like measuring a distance on a small map and then applying it directly to a large map.

533
01:14:31,452 --> 01:14:46,477
Therefore, a bidirectional coordinate scaling mechanism must be implemented at the tool layer, and the target resolution must be selected based on the aspect ratio to avoid non-uniform stretching that distorts the image and consequently biases coordinate judgment.

534
01:14:46,477 --> 01:15:05,902
For example, if the actual screen resolution is 2560×1440 (16:9), the most suitable target among Claude's three supported options is FWXGA (1366×768), which has an aspect ratio closest to 16:9.

535
01:15:05,902 --> 01:15:32,240
The screenshot is proportionally scaled to 1366×768 and fed to the model; after the model outputs the click coordinates (683, 384), they are inversely mapped to the real coordinates (683×2560/1366, 384×1440/768) ≈ (1280, 720).

536
01:15:32,240 --> 01:15:46,265
Conversely, if a 16:9 image is forcibly stretched into the 4:3 1024×768, the image will be horizontally compressed, causing the model's predicted coordinates to systematically shift.

537
01:15:46,265 --> 01:15:53,177
As illustrated in Figure 6-14: Resolution Matching and Bidirectional Coordinate Scaling.

538
01:15:53,177 --> 01:16:05,102
The choice among the three routes can be summarized as follows: when structured information is available, prioritize DOM/accessibility-tree indexing for the most accurate and stable localization.

539
01:16:05,102 --> 01:16:18,577
When it is unavailable—in native desktop software such as Photoshop, canvas/WebGL-rendered interfaces, or games—use either visual annotation (the original SoM route) or coordinate prediction.

540
01:16:18,577 --> 01:16:27,202
Visual annotation turns localization into a multiple-choice problem, making it friendlier to general-purpose models without specialized training.

541
01:16:27,202 --> 01:16:35,015
Coordinate prediction eliminates the annotation step and is more direct for models trained specifically on GUI localization.

542
01:16:35,015 --> 01:16:39,677
Both approaches still struggle with small elements and dense interfaces.

543
01:16:39,677 --> 01:16:48,077
Experiment 6-9 introductory difficulty, one star: : Using browser-use to Implement Automated Browser Operations

544
01:16:48,077 --> 01:16:56,677
Use Playwright, a browser-automation framework, together with a multimodal model to implement natural-language-driven browser operations.

545
01:16:56,677 --> 01:17:03,327
Enable SoM visualization and save a screenshot with annotated bounding boxes before every decision.

546
01:17:03,327 --> 01:17:12,377
Test task “Open Google and query San Francisco weather”: after startup, the screenshot shows Google Search with numbered interactive elements.

547
01:17:12,377 --> 01:17:21,865
The model selects the search box, enters “San Francisco weather today,” submits it, and extracts the temperature and conditions from the results page.

548
01:17:21,865 --> 01:17:26,127
A Computer Use Agent That Can Watch Animations and Hear Sound.

549
01:17:26,127 --> 01:17:37,227
So far, Computer Use perception has rested on an implicit assumption: the screen is static—take a screenshot, reason one step, click, and take the next screenshot.

550
01:17:37,227 --> 01:17:43,902
Real screens play videos, flash short-lived notifications, and carry voices from meetings.

551
01:17:43,902 --> 01:17:52,215
An Agent that opens its eyes only once every 3–5 seconds and has no ears cannot see or hear what happens between two frames.

552
01:17:52,215 --> 01:17:57,352
What needs redesign is not the action interface but the observation interface.

553
01:17:57,352 --> 01:18:06,127
An Agent–computer observation interface (AOI) converts continuous environmental observation into discrete events the model can handle.

554
01:18:06,127 --> 01:18:34,177
Its key techniques are: screen keyframe capture, which uses a small model to judge whether the screen has changed meaningfully and only takes a screenshot on a significant change—when changes are frequent, capturing once per second already works well; volume-gated speech transcription, which invokes recognition when sound is present and feeds the recognized text into the context so the Agent can hear; and describing the screen as text, so the model turns each captured screenshot into a one-

555
01:18:34,177 --> 01:18:41,752
sentence description that stays in the context after the original image leaves it, compressing multimodal interaction history.

556
01:18:41,908 --> 01:18:44,683
World Models for Computer Use.

557
01:18:44,788 --> 01:18:57,725
The observation interface of the previous section answers "what happened in between?": with keyframes, speech transcription and persistent text, the Agent no longer sees only two screenshots taken far apart.

558
01:18:57,675 --> 01:19:01,950
But an observation interface does not remove planning latency.

559
01:19:01,950 --> 01:19:11,413
The Agent is still running a serial "screenshot—think—click" loop, re-observing and reasoning about the next step after every single action.

560
01:19:11,413 --> 01:19:24,850
The OSWorld-Human efficiency study shows that even when a task eventually succeeds, the Agent takes markedly more steps and waits markedly longer than a person does; reaching human-level accuracy is not the same as being practical.

561
01:19:24,850 --> 01:19:28,988
People do not start thinking about the next step only after clicking.

562
01:19:28,988 --> 01:19:41,750
They first predict what an action will do: if the actual change matches the expectation, they carry on with the existing plan; only when the page state departs from what was expected do they stop to observe and plan again.

563
01:19:41,750 --> 01:19:51,975
A world model lets the Agent predict what the desktop may turn into before it acts, giving it this human-like "speculative execution" and improving efficiency substantially.

564
01:19:51,975 --> 01:19:55,400
Desktop state is more than a grid of pixels.

565
01:19:55,400 --> 01:20:08,725
It also includes windows, focus, scroll position, input-field contents, loading state, permissions and network responses; actions include clicking, typing, scrolling, dragging and waiting.

566
01:20:08,725 --> 01:20:19,863
A world model usable for Computer Use must at minimum encode the current state, predict the state change a candidate action would cause, and hand that prediction to the planner to decide the next step:

567
01:20:19,863 --> 01:20:27,488
Code statement: desktop state + click/type/scroll/wait ──> representation of the next state.

568
01:20:27,488 --> 01:20:39,750
This lets the Agent compare the consequences of candidate actions before it actually clicks, prepare the next step while a page is loading, and recover from a dialog that flashed past by reasoning about the state difference.

569
01:20:39,750 --> 01:21:03,825
If the task is "create a new Python file in VS Code and write hello world", the model can first predict the key state of the file tree and editor on success, and only then choose the click, type and save actions; if the task is to delete a file, it can predict inside an isolated virtual desktop whether an irreversible confirmation dialog will appear, and ask the user to confirm when necessary.

570
01:21:03,825 --> 01:21:12,900
The point here is not to have the model generate a photorealistic future screenshot, but to predict the checkable state differences that completing the task requires.

571
01:21:12,900 --> 01:21:26,438
In July 2026, Photon-1 from Induction Labs demonstrated one implementation of this route, completing the pretraining of a computer use world model with only 30,000 hours of H200 GPU time.

572
01:21:26,438 --> 01:21:44,788
It compresses each frame into discrete latent tokens and autoregressively predicts the representation of the next state after an action, rather than generating screenshots pixel by pixel during pretraining; the image generator attached to it serves only to visualize the latent representations and is not a component required for inference.

573
01:21:44,788 --> 01:21:56,813
Given a seed screenshot and the actions that follow, the model can "imagine" desktop states continuously, and then learn to output computer-use actions through online training on virtual machines.

574
01:21:56,813 --> 01:22:01,050
Mobile: Ecosystem Barriers Are Harder Than Technology.

575
01:22:01,050 --> 01:22:04,988
Computer Use is also expanding to mobile devices.

576
01:22:04,988 --> 01:22:22,650
Mobile and desktop systems do differ technically: instead of relying on mouse coordinates and keyboard input, the mobile action space typically uses the system's accessibility-service API (e.g., Android's AccessibilityService) to read interface elements and issue clicks or enter text.

577
01:22:22,650 --> 01:22:28,925
Interaction also shifts from a mouse pointer to touch gestures, changing the meaning of coordinates.

578
01:22:28,925 --> 01:22:39,375
The same (x, y) position might indicate a tap, a long press, or the starting point of a swipe, so the action must also specify a gesture type.

579
01:22:39,375 --> 01:22:49,713
Mobile benchmarks such as AndroidWorld, introduced in Chapter 7, evaluate an Agent's ability to complete tasks in real applications within this action space.

580
01:22:49,876 --> 01:22:57,388
However, what truly hinders mobile Computer Use is often not these technical differences, but ecosystem barriers.

581
01:22:57,338 --> 01:23:11,288
Some phone manufacturers have attempted to integrate AI assistants into consumer-grade phones so that the assistants can automatically operate everyday apps like WeChat, Taobao, and Alipay, but they quickly encountered platform restrictions.

582
01:23:11,288 --> 01:23:16,376
This reveals a unique challenge for Computer Use: ecosystem barriers.

583
01:23:16,376 --> 01:23:21,438
The fundamental reason behind these restrictions is a conflict of business models.

584
01:23:21,438 --> 01:23:36,151
The core monetization logic of traditional internet applications is traffic and attention: users see ads while scrolling through feeds, are guided by recommendation algorithms when searching for products, and make impulse purchases while browsing pages.

585
01:23:36,151 --> 01:23:49,313
When an Agent operates on the user's behalf, that monetization chain is bypassed entirely: the AI ignores ads, makes no impulse purchases, heads straight for the goal, finishes the task, and leaves.

586
01:23:49,313 --> 01:23:56,688
For platforms that live on advertising and traffic, every Agent operation erodes the foundation of the business model.

587
01:23:56,688 --> 01:24:04,801
This means that Computer Use faces not only technical countermeasures such as CAPTCHAs, but also a structural conflict of interest.

588
01:24:04,801 --> 01:24:12,926
This conflict will be difficult to resolve in the short term and poses a greater obstacle to consumer adoption than purely technical problems.

589
01:24:12,926 --> 01:24:17,251
Robot Manipulation: Tidying a Desk with XLeRobot.

590
01:24:17,251 --> 01:24:27,838
Reading note: This section uses one task throughout—"put the red cup in the tray, put the yellow scrap paper in the bin, then observe again and confirm the state of the desk.

591
01:24:27,838 --> 01:24:44,288
Experiments 6-10 and 6-12 run on real XLeRobot hardware and need an arm, calibration, an emergency stop and an on-site observer; experiments 6-11, 6-13 and 6-14 are the corresponding local-GPU experiments.

592
01:24:44,288 --> 01:24:52,426
Hardware and simulation are reported separately, but the task goal, the action semantics and the success conditions stay the same.

593
01:24:52,426 --> 01:24:57,201
Robot manipulation is much harder than answering questions about a picture.

594
01:24:57,201 --> 01:25:05,588
The model has to understand the scene and then take actions continuously in the real world, where every action changes what the next moment looks like.

595
01:25:05,588 --> 01:25:19,901
XLeRobot makes that difference concrete: the same arm can be teleoperated by a person through a keyboard, a gamepad or a VR device, or it can hand camera observations and a constrained set of action tools to an Agent to call on its own.

596
01:25:19,901 --> 01:25:31,563
The hardware and the task stay fixed; only the operator changes—in the first case a human observes and corrects continuously, in the second the model and the control system must do the same work.

597
01:25:31,563 --> 01:25:35,763
This section runs five experiments on "tidy the desk.

598
01:25:35,763 --> 01:25:47,538
First a human teleoperates the real XLeRobot, measuring what the hardware can do under a sufficiently capable operator; then a simulator establishes the ideal control ceiling for the same task.

599
01:25:47,538 --> 01:26:03,926
Next an Agent controls the real XLeRobot autonomously, showing how perception, planning and failure recovery affect the result; then the same tool contract goes into the simulator so that open-loop execution, step-by-step checking and world models can be compared in bulk.

600
01:26:03,926 --> 01:26:13,988
Finally the background, object appearance, lighting and visual noise change, to see whether a visual policy learned in simulation adapts to a new environment.

601
01:26:13,988 --> 01:26:23,551
The bottleneck here is usually not one more static question-answering benchmark, but whether the model can keep closing the loop under limited perception and control bandwidth.

602
01:26:23,551 --> 01:26:27,963
A usable robot system has to answer at least four questions:

603
01:26:27,963 --> 01:26:30,713
What task does the person want done?

604
01:26:30,713 --> 01:26:33,188
Which subtask comes next?

605
01:26:33,188 --> 01:26:36,551
What actions does the current skill actually emit?

606
01:26:36,551 --> 01:26:41,076
After the action executes, does reality still match the plan?

607
01:26:41,236 --> 01:27:06,198
This section places those four questions inside one XLeRobot control loop and shows what each of four techniques is responsible for: long-horizon planning decides whether the cup or the paper is handled first, a VLA or action primitive performs the grasp and the placement, a world model estimates the consequences of an action, and sim-to-real transfer handles the differences between training footage and the real camera and actuators.

608
01:27:06,198 --> 01:27:14,811
Even when the high-level model already has enough knowledge and planning ability, losing any one of these feedback links can still leave the task unfinished.

609
01:27:14,811 --> 01:27:18,811
The Division of Labour Between Hardware and Algorithms.

610
01:27:18,811 --> 01:27:30,061
The first question XLeRobot is best suited to answer is this: when autonomous desk tidying fails, is it the arm that cannot do it, or the algorithm that is not using the arm well?

611
01:27:30,061 --> 01:27:50,411
There is a fact that cannot be overlooked: an arm costing only a few hundred dollars, like XLeRobot, can already complete the kind of continuous multi-step desk task in this section through teleoperation—a person watches the camera feed, picks up the red cup and puts it in the tray, then puts the yellow scrap paper in the bin and confirms the state again.

612
01:27:50,411 --> 01:28:01,586
That result is not merely "the hardware is barely feasible"; it is a clear piece of diagnostic evidence: for this task the hardware itself is not the bottleneck, the algorithm is.

613
01:28:01,586 --> 01:28:11,573
The diagnostic method is direct: keep the camera, the arm, the gripper, the desk layout and the success conditions fixed, and let a human take over the loop.

614
01:28:11,573 --> 01:28:24,223
A human continuously corrects object localization, action choice and timing, and handles failed grasps; the gap between an autonomous system and a person lies precisely in those closed-loop abilities.

615
01:28:24,223 --> 01:28:38,548
The scope of the claim is of course this section's desk task: it shows the hardware has cleared the payload, precision and workspace thresholds this task requires, not that a few-hundred-dollar arm can handle every open environment or harder manipulation.

616
01:28:38,548 --> 01:28:45,561
XLeRobot supports keyboard, Xbox controller, Switch Joy-Con and VR teleoperation.

617
01:28:45,561 --> 01:29:02,248
A human operator naturally does many things an algorithm has to implement explicitly: slowing the gripper as it nears the cup, correcting the grasp point when the cup slides, observing again after failing to pinch the paper the first time, and checking the outcome once an object is in the target area.

618
01:29:02,248 --> 01:29:11,523
Teleoperation is therefore not only a way to collect demonstrations but also a "fix the hardware, swap the operator" diagnostic experiment.

619
01:29:11,523 --> 01:29:19,298
Experiment 6-10 introductory difficulty, one star: : Teleoperating a real XLeRobot to tidy a desk

620
01:29:19,298 --> 01:29:25,723
Place a red cup, a tray, yellow scrap paper and a bin in the real XLeRobot workspace.

621
01:29:25,723 --> 01:29:37,948
Using one calibrated teleoperation method, the operator performs the fixed task: "put the red cup in the tray, put the yellow scrap paper in the bin, then observe again and confirm the state of the desk.

622
01:29:37,948 --> 01:29:48,961
Repeat for several rounds at minimum, recording the camera feed, operator input, arm state, action timing, failed grasps, retry counts and the final state.

623
01:29:48,961 --> 01:29:53,486
Acceptance cannot rest on "the desk looks tidy at the end.

624
01:29:53,486 --> 01:30:05,873
The red cup must be inside the tray, the yellow paper inside the bin, the arm back in a safe pose, with no collision, no out-of-bounds motion and no unconfirmed manual intervention along the way.

625
01:30:05,873 --> 01:30:14,586
Teleoperation on real hardware gives the most convincing ceiling for the task, but it is not suited to varying object counts and positions in bulk.

626
01:30:14,586 --> 01:30:30,798
To obtain a repeatable, statistically meaningful control, the next step moves the same "put objects where they belong" problem into a 2D desktop simulator, using an ideal controller to stand in for a strong operator who never misperceives and never picks the wrong action.

627
01:30:30,798 --> 01:30:39,311
Experiment 6-11 introductory difficulty, one star: : Measuring the ideal control ceiling for the same task in simulation

628
01:30:39,460 --> 01:30:51,522
In a 2D desktop simulator, randomly place the red cup, the yellow paper and their target areas, and let an ideal controller approach each object in turn, grasp it and move it to the right place.

629
01:30:51,472 --> 01:31:01,472
It does not need to recognise images and never picks the wrong action, so it represents "what this task can at least achieve when perception and decision-making are both correct.

630
01:31:01,472 --> 01:31:12,047
The experiment tracks task success rate, number of steps and path length, and varies initial object positions and task scale to see whether the ideal ceiling stays stable.

631
01:31:12,047 --> 01:31:22,085
It uses the same success conditions as experiment 6-10, but measures results from an idealized simulation and does not imply the real XLeRobot has been run.

632
01:31:22,085 --> 01:31:34,072
Together the two establish the reference lines for the autonomous control that follows: experiment 6-10 is a human loop on real hardware, experiment 6-11 an ideal loop in simulation.

633
01:31:34,072 --> 01:31:37,135
The Basic Structure of Robot Control.

634
01:31:37,135 --> 01:31:41,060
Robot systems usually separate work by timescale:

635
01:31:41,060 --> 01:31:50,847
Layer: Task goal; Core question: What does the person want done; Output: "Put the cup and the paper away"; Typical timescale: Minutes.

636
01:31:50,847 --> 01:32:03,535
Layer: Long-horizon planning; Core question: What comes first, what comes after; Output: Handle the cup, then the paper, then check; Typical timescale: Seconds to minutes.

637
01:32:03,535 --> 01:32:17,697
Layer: Basic skills; Core question: Which state change to achieve now; Output: pick(red_cup), place(red_cup, tray); Typical timescale: About 1–3 s.

638
01:32:17,697 --> 01:32:32,035
Layer: VLA / skill policy; Core question: How this skill actually moves; Output: A short motion or continuous trajectory of the XLeRobot gripper; Typical timescale: About 1–10 Hz inference.

639
01:32:32,035 --> 01:32:46,810
Layer: Low-level control and safety; Core question: How to execute stably and in time; Output: Joint or end-effector commands, speed limits and emergency stop; Typical timescale: About 50–1000 Hz.

640
01:32:46,972 --> 01:32:51,722
This is a common engineering split, not the only model architecture.

641
01:32:51,672 --> 01:32:59,847
A VLA can take on part of the high-level judgement, and the planner can be a rule-based program, a VLM or an optimiser.

642
01:32:59,847 --> 01:33:16,634
Whichever implementation you choose, "task order" and "the action right now" should stay separate; otherwise the high-level model's inference latency drags down low-level control, and high-frequency low-level control forces the high-level model to process a great deal of irrelevant detail.

643
01:33:16,634 --> 01:33:32,584
For XLeRobot the model should not emit arbitrary joint angles directly; it only selects bounded skills such as pick, place, verify_state or stop, and a calibrated, speed-limited executor with timeouts turns those skills into real arm motion.

644
01:33:32,584 --> 01:33:36,234
Long-Horizon Planning and Task Decomposition.

645
01:33:36,234 --> 01:33:42,772
When the user says "tidy up the desk," the system cannot hand that sentence straight to an action model.

646
01:33:42,772 --> 01:33:53,197
The planner first lists the objects and goals in the scene, then decides the order, and for each step writes down the start condition, the completion condition and the risk limits.

647
01:33:53,197 --> 01:33:54,972
For example:

648
01:33:54,972 --> 01:33:59,947
Code statement: handle the red cup → clear the yellow paper → check the desk.

649
01:33:59,947 --> 01:34:05,322
Handle the red cup" then decomposes further into two actions and one check:

650
01:34:05,322 --> 01:34:13,172
Code statement: pick(red_cup) → place(red_cup, tray) → verify_state().

651
01:34:13,172 --> 01:34:18,059
Each completed skill provides a checkpoint where the outcome can be verified.

652
01:34:18,059 --> 01:34:31,209
If a grasp fails, only that step is retried; if someone moves an object, or the user changes the goal, only the affected later steps need replanning—the old plan does not have to be redone from scratch.

653
01:34:31,209 --> 01:34:42,747
The tools given to the agent should be equally simple: one call does one thing, the range of motion is fixed, there is a timeout, and observation happens again immediately after execution.

654
01:34:42,747 --> 01:34:53,022
Experiment 6-12 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Driving XLeRobot to tidy a desk autonomously with Gemini Robotics-ER 1.5

655
01:34:53,022 --> 01:35:03,772
Keep the real XLeRobot, the desk layout, the task instruction and the success conditions of experiment 6-10 unchanged, and replace the human operator with an Agent.

656
01:35:03,772 --> 01:35:19,147
An embodied reasoning model such as Gemini Robotics-ER 1.5 can handle observation and planning, exposing only five tools through a RoboCrew-style agent loop: observe_scene, pick, place, verify_state and stop.

657
01:35:19,147 --> 01:35:26,809
The model first observes the desk, decides the order, then calls the calibrated XLeRobot grasp and place actions.

658
01:35:26,809 --> 01:35:41,309
After every completed skill it must observe again and check the postcondition; on a failed grasp it may only retry the current skill, and it must call stop when the user says stop, when an object leaves the workspace, or when the state cannot be confirmed.

659
01:35:41,309 --> 01:35:48,522
The model cannot emit arbitrary joint angles, nor skip a real check merely because it previously said "done.

660
01:35:48,522 --> 01:35:59,972
The acceptance criteria are exactly those of experiment 6-10: cup in the tray, paper in the bin, arm back in a safe pose, no collision and no out-of-bounds motion.

661
01:35:59,972 --> 01:36:19,872
The difference is that in the autonomous experiment the task semantics must come from the model's own observation, the real actions must come from tool calls, and the final state must be confirmed by a fresh observation; the human may only start the run, hit the emergency stop and supervise safety, never complete an action on the Agent's behalf midway.

662
01:36:19,872 --> 01:36:30,384
Only then can experiments 6-10 and 6-12 be compared directly on "same hardware, same task—what is still missing between the human loop and the model loop.

663
01:36:30,384 --> 01:36:40,884
Real-hardware experiments expose calibration error, camera occlusion and gripper failure, but they are poorly suited to repeating large numbers of faults safely and controllably.

664
01:36:40,884 --> 01:36:58,147
The simulation experiments that follow keep these five tools and exactly the same task state, replacing only the real actuator with a desktop environment into which failures can be injected, in order to separate what open-loop execution, step-by-step checking and action prediction each contribute.

665
01:36:58,147 --> 01:37:00,172
VLA Control.

666
01:37:00,172 --> 01:37:03,372
VLA stands for Vision-Language-Action.

667
01:37:03,372 --> 01:37:09,859
It takes the current frame and one skill instruction, then emits the action the robot should perform next:

668
01:37:10,012 --> 01:37:14,724
Code statement: current observation + skill instruction → action.

669
01:37:14,674 --> 01:37:30,424
In the XLeRobot case the high-level planner only submits pick(red_cup); the VLA or skill policy still has to decide, from the current frame, which direction to approach the cup from, when the gripper closes and along what trajectory the arm lifts.

670
01:37:30,424 --> 01:37:41,237
After the execution layer finishes that short motion it photographs the desk again, and only once the cup is confirmed to be held may the planner submit place(red_cup, tray).

671
01:37:41,237 --> 01:37:49,449
A tool call therefore defines the desired state change, while the VLA defines how to realise that change through continuous motion.

672
01:37:49,449 --> 01:38:03,012
RT-2 and OpenVLA cut continuous actions into discrete tokens and emit them one at a time, like generating text; π₀ represents the other route, producing continuous, smooth action trajectories directly.

673
01:38:03,012 --> 01:38:12,224
Neither is simply better: discrete tokens combine more easily with language models, while continuous trajectories usually express smooth motion better.

674
01:38:12,224 --> 01:38:17,749
The real trade-off is how the action should be represented, not merely model size.

675
01:38:17,749 --> 01:38:27,062
A large model can usually run inference only 1–10 times per second, whereas a traditional controller may update tens to thousands of times per second.

676
01:38:27,062 --> 01:38:40,237
A common engineering answer is "action chunking": the model generates a short segment of future actions at once, a control thread executes that segment at a higher rate, and the model prepares the next segment in the background.

677
01:38:40,237 --> 01:38:44,587
This hides part of the inference wait inside the execution time.

678
01:38:44,587 --> 01:38:57,899
The cost is that the longer the segment, the smoother the motion but the fewer new frames the model sees during it; if the cup is knocked while XLeRobot reaches for it, the arm may still be executing actions generated from the old frame.

679
01:38:57,899 --> 01:39:04,749
Action chunking is therefore a trade-off between smoothness and reaction speed, not free acceleration.

680
01:39:04,749 --> 01:39:07,074
The Limits of VLAs.

681
01:39:07,074 --> 01:39:14,187
Long-horizon planning + VLA" is a practical baseline, but several problems are easy to overlook:

682
01:39:14,187 --> 01:39:20,362
Limited training data: robot demonstrations are far scarcer than internet text and images.

683
01:39:20,362 --> 01:39:26,849
That a model has seen the word "cup" does not mean it has seen cups of every material and friction condition.

684
01:39:26,849 --> 01:39:37,799
Imitation without consequence: behaviour cloning mainly learns "what the demonstrator did next," and never explicitly requires the model to answer "what will this action cause.

685
01:39:37,799 --> 01:39:49,287
Robots differ: different robots have different degrees of freedom, coordinate frames, grippers and actuator latencies, so the same action does not necessarily transfer to another machine.

686
01:39:49,287 --> 01:39:59,887
Observations go stale: once an action chunk starts executing, an object may be moved, occluded or knocked over while the model is still deciding from the previous frame.

687
01:39:59,887 --> 01:40:09,212
So a language model knowing what a "cup" is does not mean it knows how friction, contact, liquid sloshing and a power cable will change the future state.

688
01:40:09,212 --> 01:40:17,199
A VLA mainly answers "what should be done now"; another kind of model is needed to judge "what may happen afterwards.

689
01:40:17,199 --> 01:40:19,074
World Models.

690
01:40:19,074 --> 01:40:23,562
A world model can be understood as an "action-outcome predictor.

691
01:40:23,562 --> 01:40:29,374
What it learns is: given the current state and some action, how the next state may change.

692
01:40:29,374 --> 01:40:34,112
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

693
01:40:34,112 --> 01:40:38,949
A world model usable for robotics has to do at least three things well:

694
01:40:38,949 --> 01:40:41,324
understand the current state;

695
01:40:41,324 --> 01:40:44,587
predict the outcomes different actions may bring;

696
01:40:44,587 --> 01:40:49,037
pass those predictions to the planner or controller to help them choose.

697
01:40:49,037 --> 01:40:58,062
A VLM that can only describe video, or a model that can only generate frames, does not automatically become a reliable robot world model.

698
01:40:58,062 --> 01:41:04,112
It must also know what the actions are and be able to predict their effect on objects and the environment.

699
01:41:04,112 --> 01:41:14,137
V-JEPA 2 represents the route of predicting the future in an internal state, while World-Action Models explicitly learn the "action–future observation" relationship.

700
01:41:14,137 --> 01:41:18,924
These models can work alongside a VLA; they need not replace it.

701
01:41:18,924 --> 01:41:23,762
In practical systems a world model is typically used in three ways:

702
01:41:23,932 --> 01:41:31,282
Before acting: compare candidates such as grasping, pushing or waiting, and prefer the lower-risk option;

703
01:41:31,232 --> 01:41:39,844
During execution: compare the real observation against the prediction, and on divergence shorten the action, stop, or replan;

704
01:41:39,844 --> 01:41:49,207
During training: learn state transitions from video, simulation data and failure trajectories, reducing trial and error on real hardware.

705
01:41:49,207 --> 01:42:02,807
Back to the XLeRobot desk task: if the yellow paper is partly hidden under the red cup, the system can compare candidate skills such as "grab the paper first," "move the cup first" and "approach from another direction.

706
01:42:02,807 --> 01:42:14,519
The world model does not need to generate photorealistic robot video; predicting which candidates are more likely to make the paper graspable and which might knock the cup over is already enough to help the planner rank them.

707
01:42:14,519 --> 01:42:24,457
Once an action executes, the real camera observation remains the final truth; prediction can inform the choice but cannot replace checking that the task succeeded.

708
01:42:24,457 --> 01:42:31,619
What a world model gives is not a definite answer but a comparable prediction of "if I do this, what may happen.

709
01:42:31,619 --> 01:42:40,919
The further ahead it predicts, the larger the error usually grows, and a future frame that looks realistic may still violate real contact and friction.

710
01:42:40,919 --> 01:42:50,794
Practical systems therefore still need short-horizon prediction, real-time observation, an estimate of uncertainty, and an independent hardware safety controller.

711
01:42:50,794 --> 01:43:00,594
Generative world models can serve interactive simulation or visualisation, but "can generate video" must not be conflated with "can guide robot action.

712
01:43:00,594 --> 01:43:09,169
Experiment 6-13 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Comparing three autonomous desk-tidying loops in simulation

713
01:43:09,169 --> 01:43:26,282
Put the task, object state, success conditions and five tools of experiment 6-12 into the desktop simulator unchanged, replacing only the real XLeRobot actuator with a controllable simulated one, and let grasps occasionally suffer recoverable transient failures.

714
01:43:26,282 --> 01:43:30,807
This allows three strategies to be compared without changing the problem.

715
01:43:30,807 --> 01:43:50,332
Open-loop execution generates the full action sequence once and never observes again midway; step-by-step checking re-reads the state after every pick and place and retries only the current skill on failure; predictive execution adds a short-horizon world model, comparing the expected outcomes of candidate skills before choosing the next step.

716
01:43:50,332 --> 01:44:01,719
The experiment compares task success rate, tool-call overhead and failure-recovery ability, and checks that every final success is confirmed by a fresh verify_state observation.

717
01:44:01,719 --> 01:44:20,119
The point is not to prove that a small simulated world model equals a real robot's physics model, but to verify a more basic relationship: an open-loop plan carries a single local failure all the way to the end of the task, step-by-step checking can recover, and action prediction can further help rank candidate skills.

718
01:44:20,119 --> 01:44:25,344
Whether the task is truly finished must still be decided by environment feedback.

719
01:44:25,344 --> 01:44:28,319
From Simulation to a Real Robot.

720
01:44:28,319 --> 01:44:37,732
Even if experiment 6-13 is stable in the simulator, that does not imply the real XLeRobot of experiment 6-12 will succeed the same way.

721
01:44:37,732 --> 01:44:46,269
Going from simulation to a real robot is not a matter of swapping in yet another controller, but of handling the differences between two environments.

722
01:44:46,269 --> 01:45:06,107
Training may use teleoperation data, video data or simulated interaction data; in real deployment the same red cup, yellow paper, tray and bin appear against different backgrounds, lighting, camera positions and occlusion relationships, and the arm additionally meets different friction, sensor noise and actuator latency.

723
01:45:06,107 --> 01:45:12,007
Once those differences are large enough, motions learned in simulation may fail in reality.

724
01:45:12,007 --> 01:45:20,257
Experiment 6-14 advanced difficulty, three stars: : A cross-environment RGB test on the same desk task

725
01:45:20,428 --> 01:45:35,728
Keep using the basic "move the object to its target" problem in simulation, treating each sample as one local decision within desk tidying: from the RGB frame, judge which direction to approach the object from, or whether it can already be grasped.

726
01:45:35,678 --> 01:45:49,003
Train four visual policies with identical structure: one sees only a fixed scene, one varies the background, one varies object appearance, and the last varies background, appearance, lighting and noise together.

727
01:45:49,003 --> 01:45:58,353
All policies are tested in the original environment and in the changed one, comparing action-decision accuracy before and after the visual conditions change.

728
01:45:58,353 --> 01:46:12,578
The question here is not "is the simulator already equal to the real XLeRobot," but a narrower one: does actively widening the range of visual variation during training help the same cup–tray, paper–bin task adapt to a new camera view?

729
01:46:12,578 --> 01:46:20,978
Even if the result improves, real deployment still requires real camera calibration, actuator testing and a complete safety loop.

730
01:46:20,978 --> 01:46:22,865
Chapter Summary.

731
01:46:22,865 --> 01:46:38,703
Viewed along the two axes of modality and execution timing, asynchrony and event-driven execution expand observation from “the Agent fetches it” to “the world pushes it,” and action from “finish within the turn” to “start now and finish through later events.

732
01:46:38,703 --> 01:46:49,028
Voice compresses the scale to milliseconds, moving from turn-taking toward continuous listening and speaking while dividing realtime foreground interaction from deeper background thought.

733
01:46:49,028 --> 01:46:58,553
Computer Use moves the loop to the screen, where the bottlenecks include efficiency, continuous visual understanding, and state confirmation after actions.

734
01:46:58,553 --> 01:47:07,953
Robotics moves it into the physical world, where action chunking trades smoothness against responsiveness and completion must still be judged from a new observation.

735
01:47:07,953 --> 01:47:11,515
The four sections share one control skeleton:

736
01:47:11,515 --> 01:47:16,253
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

737
01:47:16,253 --> 01:47:25,078
They also share the same primitives—wake-up, safe points, cancellation, preemption, and fast/slow separation.

738
01:47:25,228 --> 01:47:36,953
This chapter completes the last piece of the “building an Agent” part: the observation and action spaces have now been expanded in all three directions—content, modality, and timing.

739
01:47:36,903 --> 01:47:53,378
Next, Chapter 7 asks how to determine whether the system was built correctly; Chapter 8 explains how post-training updates model parameters; and Chapter 9 organizes runtime trajectories, evaluation, and multiple update carriers into a continual-evolution loop.

740
01:47:53,378 --> 01:47:59,253
Chapter 10 then moves from this complete single-Agent foundation to multi-Agent collaboration.

741
01:47:59,253 --> 01:48:01,190
Thought Questions.

742
01:48:01,190 --> 01:48:10,728
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  In an asynchronous Agent architecture, the priority strategy for the event queue must be determined at design time.

743
01:48:10,728 --> 01:48:23,365
But if priority judgment itself requires semantic understanding (e.g., determining whether a new message is more urgent than the current task), who should make this judgment—a rules engine or another LLM call?

744
01:48:23,365 --> 01:48:25,590
What are the costs of each?

745
01:48:25,590 --> 01:48:33,390
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  In queue-based event processing, models tend to focus only on the last event.

746
01:48:33,390 --> 01:48:38,603
This chapter mitigates this through Agent status bar markers and summarization.

747
01:48:38,603 --> 01:48:51,815
But if the queue has 20 events backlogged (10 tool results + 5 user messages + 5 system alerts), how would you organize the presentation order and format of these events so that the model does not miss key information?

748
01:48:51,815 --> 01:49:10,203
advanced difficulty, three stars:  When an Agent interacts with the external world on behalf of a user, it essentially faces an identity choice: use an independent virtual identity (dedicated email and phone number) to act as a third party, or directly operate the user's personal accounts as the user?

749
01:49:10,203 --> 01:49:23,790
The former allows autonomous background operation, but third parties may not trust a non-human identity; the latter has more complete context and permissions but introduces authorization, trust, and security-boundary issues.

750
01:49:23,790 --> 01:49:27,515
In what scenarios do you think each mode should be chosen?

751
01:49:27,515 --> 01:49:38,853
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  The end-to-end model for voice Agents merges ASR-LLM-TTS into a single model, reducing latency but losing modularity.

752
01:49:38,853 --> 01:49:48,453
If the end-to-end model makes an error in a specific stage (e.g., speech recognition), debugging and fixing it is much harder than in a serial pipeline.

753
01:49:48,453 --> 01:49:53,190
How would you design an observability system for an end-to-end voice Agent?

754
01:49:53,190 --> 01:50:01,953
introductory difficulty, one star:  Step-Audio R1 achieves "thinking while speaking" through the MPS dual-brain architecture.

755
01:50:01,953 --> 01:50:11,440
However, humans, when "thinking while speaking," often say things before they have fully thought them through, self-correct, or use filler words.

756
01:50:11,440 --> 01:50:16,315
Should an Agent's "thinking while speaking" mimic these human characteristics?

757
01:50:16,315 --> 01:50:37,328
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  SoM (Set-of-Mark) and its structured variants (DOM element indexing) convert Computer Use's visual localization from open-ended coordinate prediction to closed-set ID selection, but they all require detecting and annotating UI elements first—whether via a segmentation model or the DOM.

758
01:50:37,328 --> 01:50:45,465
If the interface contains non-standard controls or dynamically changing elements, the annotations may be incomplete or inaccurate.

759
01:50:45,465 --> 01:50:49,715
In such cases, should we fall back to coordinate prediction?

760
01:50:49,715 --> 01:51:00,078
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  Robot platforms like XLeRobot, costing a few hundred dollars, make teleoperation data collection inexpensive.

761
01:51:00,078 --> 01:51:05,803
However, the quality of teleoperation data depends heavily on the operator's skill.

762
01:51:05,803 --> 01:51:11,403
How would low-quality data from an unskilled operator affect the training of a VLA model?

763
01:51:11,403 --> 01:51:16,490
How can low-quality data be automatically filtered during the data collection phase?

764
01:51:16,660 --> 01:51:25,585
advanced difficulty, three stars:  This chapter covers three forms of interaction: voice, Computer Use, and robotics.

765
01:51:25,535 --> 01:51:38,497
Interaction architectures can improve through end-to-end integration, modular cascades, or separation of foreground interaction from background reasoning, without requiring a corresponding increase in reasoning capability.

766
01:51:38,497 --> 01:51:48,247
Over the next five years, should Agents prioritize stronger unified models or retain separate, replaceable models for fast interaction and deeper reasoning?

767
01:51:48,247 --> 01:51:55,122
Discuss the trade-offs in latency, observability, the pace of model upgrades, and task risk.

768
01:51:55,122 --> 01:52:17,260
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  DOM/Accessibility Tree element indexing works well on standard web applications, but an increasing number of software interfaces (Canvas/WebGL rendering, cross-platform custom-drawn controls) do not provide accessible structured information, relying solely on visual annotation or coordinate prediction.

769
01:52:17,260 --> 01:52:24,147
Do you think Computer Use should bet on a purely visual approach, or maintain both structured and visual paths?

770
01:52:24,147 --> 01:52:28,085
What are the costs and benefits of maintaining both paths?

771
01:52:28,085 --> 01:52:44,460
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  VLA models use action chunking—as mentioned in the text, the model generates a short sequence of future actions at once, and a control thread executes them at a higher rate—to hide inference latency within execution time.

772
01:52:44,460 --> 01:52:53,747
However, if the environment changes suddenly during execution (e.g., an object is moved), the pre-generated action sequence becomes invalid.

773
01:52:53,747 --> 01:53:00,510
How can we balance the efficiency advantage of action chunking with the need for responsiveness to environmental changes?

774
01:53:00,510 --> 01:53:15,885
advanced difficulty, three stars:  All three scenarios in this chapter—voice, Computer Use, and robotics—face latency in the "perceive-think-act" loop and need to divide responsibilities between deeper reasoning and timely interaction.

775
01:53:15,885 --> 01:53:27,735
In voice, this manifests as "correcting after misspeaking"; in Computer Use, as "clicking first, then looking"; in robotics, as "taking a step, then looking.

776
01:53:27,735 --> 01:53:40,760
How can classifying actions by risk, using reversible operations, verifying state, enforcing permission controls, and stopping safely prevent fast interaction from causing irreversible consequences?

777
01:53:40,760 --> 01:53:54,047
advanced difficulty, three stars:  The same set of primitives (wake-up, safe point, cancellation, preemption, fast/slow separation) recurs in this chapter at different time scales.

778
01:53:54,047 --> 01:54:03,310
Pick one and explain how its implementation differs between event-driven processing (seconds to days) and robot action chunking (milliseconds).

779
01:54:03,310 --> 01:54:12,422
What mainly determines that difference—the speed at which the environment changes, the reversibility of the action, or the cost of obtaining an observation?

